"system:partition over a REAL COMPILED STORE, and the store is inlined here because it turned out to be 372 characters. I twice reported that these store-shaped DEFs needed a fixture harness the case table could not provide, and twice that was wrong: compiling 'Person places Order' answers FIVE cells -- role, factType, refMode, instanceOf, FILE -- which is smaller than several operands already in this file. My probes failed because I GUESSED the cells (a functional population, an smDef population) instead of compiling three lines and looking. The answer is Halpin rule 1: a fact type with no single-role UC gets its OWN table, so the pair is <Person_places_Order, Person_places_Order> -- ft and table equal, which is exactly the f == t case rmap:abs_targets drops.",
DEF("case:partition-compiled", S2(A("system:partition"),
    S5(S3(A("CELL"), A("role"),
          S2(S4(A("Person_places_Order.2"), A("Person_places_Order"), N(2), A("Order")),
             S4(A("Person_places_Order.1"), A("Person_places_Order"), N(1), A("Person")))),
       S3(A("CELL"), A("factType"),
          S1(S2(A("Person_places_Order"), A("{0} places {1}")))),
       S3(A("CELL"), A("refMode"),
          S2(S2(A("Person"), A("name")), S2(A("Order"), A("id")))),
       S3(A("CELL"), A("instanceOf"),
          S2(S2(A("Person"), A("ObjectType")), S2(A("Order"), A("ObjectType")))),
       S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), PHI())))),

"THE _spec VARIANTS were the only three DEFs in the whole canon that the host calls and NO signal touched -- no case, no law, and unlike the other thirty-one on that list, no pytest either. They differ from their plain siblings by taking a <kind, name> SPEC where those take a bare name: constraints:pop_of_spec dispatches on kind == 'cell', so the spec form is the one that can also address a view. Same three shapes as the plain cases above -- violation, no-violation, and for equality the right-only asymmetry -- because the point is that the spec form agrees with the form it shadows.",
DEF("case:subset-spec-violates", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("constraints:scoped_subset_spec")),
                K(S2(A("cell"), A("Person"))))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("c"))),
       S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("Person"), S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b")))))))),
DEF("case:mandatory-facts-spec-violates", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("constraints:scoped_mandatory_facts_spec")),
                K(S2(A("cell"), A("F"))))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S3(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b")), S1(A("c"))),
       S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("F"), S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b")))))))),
DEF("case:equality-side-spec-right-only", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("constraints:scoped_equality_side_spec")),
                K(S2(A("cell"), A("F"))))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S1(S1(A("b"))),
       S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("F"), S2(S1(A("b")), S1(A("c")))))))),

"system:machine_step and system:mealy_step build the UNREDUCED application a create record carries as data (main.rs 9225, 9233) -- reached by nothing. Operand <ft, rc> where rc is PHI or a one-element <col>, which is Rust's Option<i64> crossing into canon as an empty-or-singleton sequence rather than as a null. The DEFs' leading COND tests exactly that emptiness, so the two arms ARE the None and Some cases and casing one would leave the dispatch half-checked. These pin the built term, not its behavior: the term is invoked later, against the addressed entity's row, when the assembled handler meets <fact, D> -- the comment at 9215 says so and the case cannot reach that far.",
DEF("case:machine-step-nocol", S2(A("system:machine_step"), S2(A("F1"), PHI()))),
DEF("case:machine-step-col", S2(A("system:machine_step"), S2(A("F1"), S1(N(2))))),
DEF("case:mealy-step-nocol", S2(A("system:mealy_step"), S2(A("F1"), PHI()))),
DEF("case:mealy-step-col", S2(A("system:mealy_step"), S2(A("F1"), S1(N(2))))),

"system:sm_rows is the state-machine handlers' shared row object, host-called at compile.rs 2235, reached by nothing. Operand <verb, head, group1, group2> -- four bare strings, which is why this one is caseable where system:partition beside it is not. The verb is the recognizer token the HANDLER passes, and it dispatches. These use the LIVE verbs: compiler.py 1421-1442 passes exactly eight -- 'is for Noun', 'is initial in State Machine Definition', 'is from Status', 'is to Status', 'is triggered by Fact Type', 'is guarded by Fact Type', and 'emits' twice. My first version of the smdef case used 'is for Object Type', taken from the grammar document rather than from a caller; it answered rows, because system:sm_rows has an arm for it, but NO HANDLER PASSES THAT STRING -- the case pinned an arm the compiler never reaches. Corrected here to the verb _h_sm_def actually sends -- and correcting it EXPOSED A REAL DEFECT, because it then answered phi. system:sm_rows keyed its sm_def arm on 'is for Object Type', which NO caller passes, so no smDef row was ever produced on any lineage. docs/04-state-machines.md:10, metamodel/state.md:173, the grammar, both hosts and three tests all say 'is for Noun'; metamodel/state.md:26 says 'is for Object Type' but that declares the M-LEVEL metamodel fact type, which is not the name a reading generates -- misreading one for the other cost two failed attempts. THREE COUPLED SITES carried the wrong string: the arm's guard, the fact-type name it builds, and system:h_sm_def's own copy. Fixing them one at a time only moved which test failed. All three corrected here; test_sm_canon and test_sm_handler_canon go 3 red to 8 green in 2.8 SECONDS, having been red throughout a session whose gate never ran them. The emits pair is why the head is an operand at all: ONE verb, two heads, Transition for the Mealy form and Status for the Moore, and the rows differ accordingly. -unknown is the arm that matters: a verb no handler recognizes must not produce rows, because a compile that accepted rows for an unrecognized reading would write facts nobody declared. Casing the dispatch AND its fall-through is the pair; either alone leaves half the decision unchecked.",
DEF("case:sm-rows-smdef", S2(A("system:sm_rows"),
    S4(A("is for Noun"), A("State Machine Definition"), A("OrderSM"), A("Order")))),
DEF("case:sm-rows-emits-mealy", S2(A("system:sm_rows"),
    S4(A("emits"), A("Transition"), A("t1"), A("E1")))),
DEF("case:sm-rows-emits-moore", S2(A("system:sm_rows"),
    S4(A("emits"), A("Status"), A("s1"), A("E1")))),
DEF("case:sm-rows-unknown", S2(A("system:sm_rows"),
    S4(A("no such verb"), A("h"), A("g1"), A("g2")))),

"system:table_columns is host-called all through op_sql_project (mf_table_columns, main.rs 7288) and was reached by nothing. It is applied to the TABLE NAME first and the resulting term to the partition pairs -- napp(napp(name, table), pairs) at 7290, two applications, not one call with two arguments. That is the shape to get right and it is only visible at the call site. The pairs are plain <ft, table> rows, so unlike system:partition next to it this needs NO compiled model: the operand is writable by hand, which is why this one could be cased and partition could not. -other answers phi for a table nothing absorbs into, and ORDER is the pairs' own -- F1 before F2 because the pairs list says so, not because anything sorted them, which is what makes the column positions line up with the DDL downstream.",
DEF("case:table-columns", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:table_columns")), K(A("T1")))),
          A("id"))),
    S3(S2(A("F1"), A("T1")), S2(A("F2"), A("T1")), S2(A("F3"), A("T2"))))),
DEF("case:table-columns-other", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:table_columns")), K(A("T9")))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S2(A("F1"), A("T1")), S2(A("F2"), A("T1"))))),

"CORRECTION TO THE FIX PROPOSED BELOW. I wrote that store:replace_cell should take a cell name and a value rather than a store, and let the host splice. That is the WRONG DIRECTION and it took reading it back to see: replacement is meaning, and moving it to the host to save marshalling is exactly the drift this whole exercise removes. A DEF operand shape IS a cost decision, which stands -- but the answer is not to shrink the DEF, it is to stop paying twice on the host side. reduce_ev converts V to N per call, and when the operand IS the store the host ALREADY HOLDS IT AS N in srv.nd and srv.ncells. Passing that through without a round trip keeps the meaning in canon and removes the store-sized cost. Optimizing a correctness property into a performance one is the failure mode this repo is built against, and I proposed it after a hundred increments of removing it.", "HOISTED, AND ONLY THIRTEEN PER CENT -- so the index rebuild was NOT the dominant cost. op_sql_project now builds one NEval and reuses it for all sixteen of its calls, safe because its srv is &Srv and cells provably cannot change; 95.0s to 82.4s warm, answer byte-identical at 208210 bytes. Eighteen full scans of a 965-cell store were worth an eighth of the runtime and the other eighty-two seconds are elsewhere. THE NEXT SUSPECT IS MARSHALLING, and it is partly self-inflicted: every call still converts its operands V to N and the answer back, and FIVE sites pass the WHOLE CELL LIST as an operand -- the store:replace_cell wirings this session added in scheduler_cells and layout_cells, three and two. Each is a full store round trip, on the COMPILE path where a sql_project timing cannot see it. Forty-four reduce_over_n sites remain. The canon-first work and the cost defect compound in two distinct ways: more calls, and bigger operands per call, and the second is the one that scales with the store.", "NINETEEN, AND ZERO. op_sql_project makes NINETEEN reduce_over_n calls and uses ev.mu ZERO times, so it constructs nineteen separate NEvals and rebuilds the name index nineteen times, each a full scan of the 965-cell store. Many of those calls are this session moving meaning into canon one DEF at a time, so the canon-first work and the cost defect compound directly. THE SAFE FIX IS LOCAL: build ONE NEval at the top of the op and reuse it for all nineteen. That needs no Srv-level cache and therefore no invalidation -- the NEval is local and its cells really are immutable for its life, which is precisely what the index comment assumes. The Srv-level variant is the one to avoid: eighteen sites assign srv.ncells, and a cache hoisted there needs every one of them to invalidate it, where a single miss gives stale cell lookups and silently wrong answers -- the same shape of defect as everything else this session found.", "AND WHERE THE COST IS. NEval carries an index -- name to the position of the FIRST cell with that name -- and its comment states the intent: built once, lazily, because cells is immutable for an NEval whole life. But reduce_over_n CONSTRUCTS A FRESH NEval PER CALL with an empty index, so an NEval life is one reduction and the index is rebuilt from nothing every time, scanning the 965-cell store to do it. op_sql_project makes dozens of those calls, several of which this session added. The fix implied by the comment is to hoist the index to Srv beside ncells, which it indexes, so it is built once per STORE rather than once per CALL -- and Srv already refreshes ncells at every site that replaces the retained store, which is the invariant such a cache needs. Not attempted here: it is the hot path, it wants a byte-identical derived-state control and a timing measured against a warm base, and diagnosing is cheaper than guessing at it with the room left. The C64 reading in this repo prescribes exactly this -- memo pure applications in the head, certify byte-identical.", "THE EVALUATOR COST, MEASURED. op sql_project over the 965-cell base takes 95 SECONDS with the native theta arms on, and 160 seconds with AREST_NO_THETA_ARMS=1. Two numbers follow. First, the certified twins buy about forty per cent -- real, and not the difference between this and a usable system. Second, and this is the one that matters, the OPTIMIZED path still takes ninety-five seconds to project a thousand cells. The C64 reading in this repo says a long leg indicts the evaluator, and ninety-five seconds for a projection this size is not a long leg, it is a different order of computing. The concrete damage is already recorded elsewhere in this session: a test run that takes fifteen minutes and an op suite that takes twenty forced a gate of three files, and that gate hid a 2.8-second failing test for an entire session while state machines were inert on every lineage. The cost is not a performance backlog item beside the correctness work. It is the reason the correctness work was invisible.", "THE CERTIFIED TWINS ARE VERIFIED EQUAL, and the unit tests structurally cannot do it. engine/rust recognizes theta:NatJoin, dedup, member, flatten, append_phi and join_combine as native arms; AREST_NO_THETA_ARMS=1 disables every one and falls back to the canon DEFs. Running op sql_project over the base both ways gives BYTE-IDENTICAL output, 208210 bytes, cmp silent -- so on that path the overrides are what the registry claims. The unit tests cannot check this because they run tools/rust-host, which is the THIN evaluator and has no arms to disable; only engine/rust has them, and only the op surface reaches it. A twin that had drifted would show here and nowhere else in the repo. Method note, because it cost two wrong readings: compare the SAME configuration twice before comparing two configurations. The first invocation after a build differs from later ones -- the base_seed thaw is warm or cold -- so an md5 over the whole transcript reports a difference that has nothing to do with the variable under test.", "AND THE REMAINING NATIVE SURFACE IS ACCOUNTED FOR. Eleven functions in engine/rust end in _native. NINE are called from op_compile_model, directly or through compile_lines and status_facts -- machine_fold, generator_cells, compile_lines, layout_cells, status_facts, create_handlers, replay_entries, rekey_transitions, scheduler_cells -- which puts them in the compiler, which AREST.tex line 332 places outside DEFS with the evaluator, parser and verbalizer. ev_cols is reached only through ov_system_ev_cols, the override mechanism, and classify_heads is on the evaluator path and is the registered twin of system:classify_heads. NOT ONE of the eleven is unsanctioned meaning. Taken with the 74-name primitive diff above, the question the loop asks -- which capability does fat rust have that thin rust lacks and that belongs in canon -- has no remaining instance. What is left is machinery by the paper's division, twins by the registry, and the evaluator cost, which is a defect of a different kind.", "WHAT THE THIN/FAT GAP ACTUALLY IS, measured rather than sampled. The two rust primitive tables differ by 74 names: host 25, engine 99. Every one of the 74 falls into four groups -- the MCP op surface (ask, cells, query, schema, verify, apps_*, tutor_*), the translator dispatch (translate_nouns, translate_fact_types, translate_state_machines and 29 more), the compile kinds (fact_type_reading, subtype_of, uniqueness, mandatory, ...), and the scheduler pass names (agg, keyed, sweep, dred, aggwhole). NONE of them is a missing primitive and none is meaning that belongs in canon. AREST.tex line 332 settles it -- the evaluator, parser, compiler, verbalizer and registered functions are unchanged by a schema-extension step -- those four are FIXED MACHINERY, not DEFS. Thin rust is the evaluator; fat rust is the evaluator plus parser, compiler, verbalizer and op surface. The translator TABLE is already data: the grammar declares 32 Classification-has-Translator facts and the selfhost path dispatches from them, so what the host holds is the implementations the table points at, which is machinery by the paper's own division. The gap that remains is certified twins and genuine meaning, not these 74.", "THE DECLARED BOUNDARY IS FIVE; THE IRREDUCIBLE ONE IS TWO. Of the five system:registered names, THREE have canon DEFs -- escape_html, slug, strip_prefix -- so for those the registration is a performance OVERRIDE, not a dependency, exactly like the theta arms in the twin registry. Only implode and lex have no canon definition and are genuinely host-only. That distinction is invisible in the constant and it matters: engine/rust carries strip_prefix natively and tools/rust-host does NOT, which looks like a thin/fat gap until you notice the canon DEF covers the host. slug preserves CASE (SQLite_Fact_Base, not sqlite_fact_base) -- rmap:ddl_name applies strdown separately, which is why its guard case reads t_sqlite_fact_base. case:strip-prefix-miss already pins the arm rmap:ddl_name's guard depends on -- an absent prefix returns the INPUT UNCHANGED, not phi and not bottom -- and case:slug-reading already shows the case preservation. I wrote three cases duplicating those before checking, having misread a per-family DEF-name count as a coverage count; they are removed. The 5-versus-2 distinction above is what was actually missing, and it is a note, not a case.",
"COROLLARY boundary, EQUATION eq:boundary, CASED. AREST.tex: the host-supplied semantic surface is Filter(eq . <s_origin, 'registered'>) : DEFS, 'the point at which unchecked general computation may re-enter the semantics'. system:registered IS that enumeration and it was uncased. THE ANSWER IS FIVE, AND ALL FIVE ARE TEXT: implode, escape_html, slug, lex, strip_prefix. Everything else in DEFS is compiled and total for finite P (Definition reg). This is the sharpest statement of the thin/fat gap the loop is about -- a host whose canon carried a SIXTH entry would admit host code the others do not, and the boundary would differ per platform while every law still held. Byte-identical agreement on this one constant is what makes 'every platform is equal' checkable rather than asserted. It also names the Stage-1 seed exactly: text-to-atom is the informal boundary BECAUSE these five, and only these five, are registered.",
DEF("case:registered-boundary", S2(A("system:registered"), PHI())),

"system:verbalize answers every fact MENTIONING an entity, as <template, row> pairs -- the primary reading of a compiled object (AREST.tex cites halpin06 for the round trip, and Proposition spec rests on it: nf(nf) = nf because re-parsing a verbalization returns the same compiled object). Applied twice, (verbalize : id) : D, per main.rs 3361. Note the SPLIT: canon supplies the template and the binding, the host substitutes {N} -- Stage-1 text is the host's seed boundary, so verbalize stops exactly where formatting begins. -role2 is the discriminating case: c1 plays the SECOND role and must verbalize the same fact, because the relation is 'mentions', not 'is the subject of'. An implementation scanning role 1 alone passes -role1 and -absent and silently makes half the facts about an entity invisible to it.",
DEF("case:verbalize-role1", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:verbalize")), K(A("o1")))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("factType"),
          S1(S2(A("Order_is_paid_by_Customer"), A("{0} is paid by {1}")))),
       S3(A("CELL"), A("Order_is_paid_by_Customer"), S1(S2(A("o1"), A("c1"))))))),
DEF("case:verbalize-role2", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:verbalize")), K(A("c1")))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("factType"),
          S1(S2(A("Order_is_paid_by_Customer"), A("{0} is paid by {1}")))),
       S3(A("CELL"), A("Order_is_paid_by_Customer"), S1(S2(A("o1"), A("c1"))))))),
DEF("case:verbalize-absent", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:verbalize")), K(A("zz")))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("factType"),
          S1(S2(A("Order_is_paid_by_Customer"), A("{0} is paid by {1}")))),
       S3(A("CELL"), A("Order_is_paid_by_Customer"), S1(S2(A("o1"), A("c1"))))))),

"THEOREM hateoas, EQUATION eq:links, CASED. AREST.tex: links(e) = nav(e) union transitions(status(e)) = Adm(e) -- the emitted controls are sound and complete for the operational command relation. nav_of and transitions_of are cased separately elsewhere in this file; these two pin the UNION, which is what the theorem is about. -active carries an entity in 'In Cart': its nav rows AND the one transition leaving that status. -terminal carries an entity in 'Shipped': nav rows and NOTHING ELSE, because no transition leaves a terminal status. THAT IS THE SOUNDNESS HALF. An implementation appending the whole machine to every entity satisfies -active and offers, on -terminal, an affordance the model does not admit -- a control the command relation would refuse. Fielding fixed hypermedia as a constraint and left the controls to be hand-written; the paper's claim is that they are GENERATED, and a generated set that over-offers is not the same claim.",
DEF("case:links-of-active", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:links_of")),
                K(S3(N(1), S2(S3(A("In Cart"), A("pay"), A("Paid")),
                              S3(A("Paid"), A("ship"), A("Shipped"))), N(2))))),
          A("id"))),
    S3(S2(A("o1"), A("In Cart")), S2(A("o2"), A("Paid")), S2(A("o1"), A("In Cart"))))),
DEF("case:links-of-terminal", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:links_of")),
                K(S3(N(1), S2(S3(A("In Cart"), A("pay"), A("Paid")),
                              S3(A("Paid"), A("ship"), A("Shipped"))), N(2))))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S2(A("o1"), A("Shipped")), S2(A("o2"), A("Paid"))))),

"POLARITY IS THE ONLY PARAMETER. AREST.tex section 2: every constraint compiles to a restriction whose predicate falls in one of two families -- a cardinality count against declared bounds, or a membership test against a target population -- 'with polarity the only parameter separating symmetric from asymmetric and subset from exclusion'. These two cases carry the SAME rows and the SAME store and differ only in which constraint is applied: subset, which says a member must BE in Q, flags a; exclusion, which says a member must NOT share, flags b. Together they PARTITION the population. Every other constraint case in this file pins one builder against one operand; this pair pins the claim the paper actually makes, and a polarity flip -- the defect that produces plausible-looking violations and passes every single-builder case -- shows up here as the two answers swapping.",
DEF("case:polarity-subset", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("constraints:scoped_subset")), K(A("Q")))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b"))),
       S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("P"), S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("Q"), S2(S1(A("b")), S1(A("c")))))))),
DEF("case:polarity-exclusion", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("constraints:scoped_exclusion")),
                K(S2(S2(A("P"), A("Q")), A("P"))))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b"))),
       S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("P"), S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("Q"), S2(S1(A("b")), S1(A("c")))))))),

"system:compile_agg_rule builds the aggregate rule, stratified above the closure and fully recomputed (compiler.py 2089, so no ~d variants). Host-called and reached by nothing. Operand <aspecs, group_cols, over_col, op, filters>; the term applies to the store and fetches its atom's population. THE THREE CASES SHARE ONE OPERAND EXCEPT FOR op AND ONE STORE, which is what makes them worth having together: sum answers <g1,5> and <g2,5>, count answers <g1,2> and <g2,1>, max answers <g1,3> and <g2,5>. An implementation ignoring op fails two of the three whichever one it picked. Note g1's sum and g2's max are both 5 by construction -- the answers are not separable by a single row, only by the whole shape. The grouping is real: two groups from three rows.",
DEF("case:agg-rule-sum", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:compile_agg_rule")),
                K(S5(S1(S3(A("F1"), N(1), PHI())), S1(N(1)), N(2), A("sum"), PHI())))),
          A("id"))),
    S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("F1"),
          S3(S2(A("g1"), N(2)), S2(A("g1"), N(3)), S2(A("g2"), N(5))))))),
DEF("case:agg-rule-count", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:compile_agg_rule")),
                K(S5(S1(S3(A("F1"), N(1), PHI())), S1(N(1)), N(2), A("count"), PHI())))),
          A("id"))),
    S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("F1"),
          S3(S2(A("g1"), N(2)), S2(A("g1"), N(3)), S2(A("g2"), N(5))))))),
DEF("case:agg-rule-max", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:compile_agg_rule")),
                K(S5(S1(S3(A("F1"), N(1), PHI())), S1(N(1)), N(2), A("max"), PHI())))),
          A("id"))),
    S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("F1"),
          S3(S2(A("g1"), N(2)), S2(A("g1"), N(3)), S2(A("g2"), N(5))))))),

"system:validate_of builds validate_S: <P, D> to <P, V, alethicViolated>. Host-called (engine.py 870) and reached by nothing. Operand is <local, alethic?, scoped, scoped_alethic?>. AN ABSENT SUBSET AND A PROVIDED EMPTY ONE ARE DIFFERENT THINGS and that is the whole point of these cases: absent means every constraint is alethic, the default, so a violation BLOCKS the commit; provided-empty means none are, deliberately deontic, so the identical violation is recorded and the write PROCEEDS. -default and -empty differ in the operand only by <> versus <<>>, carry the same V, and answer T versus F. An implementation normalizing absence to emptiness -- the obvious simplification, and one no shape check would notice -- allows every write that should have been blocked. engine.py:849 states the rule; these two make a host prove it.",
DEF("case:validate-of-alethic-default", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:validate_of")),
                K(S4(S1(S2(A("CONST"), S1(S1(A("x"))))), PHI(), PHI(), PHI())))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b"))), PHI()))),
DEF("case:validate-of-alethic-empty", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:validate_of")),
                K(S4(S1(S2(A("CONST"), S1(S1(A("x"))))), S1(PHI()), PHI(), PHI())))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b"))), PHI()))),
DEF("case:validate-of-clean", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:validate_of")),
                K(S4(PHI(), PHI(), PHI(), PHI())))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b"))), PHI()))),

"system:sm_join assembles the machine from M: smFrom <t,from> joined smTrigger <t,trigger> joined smTo <t,to>, projected to <from, trigger, to>. engine.py:1567 puts it plainly -- a machine IS a set of facts, and assembling it is a theta1 join over M's cells, not a second interpreter. Host-called (sm_triples) and reached by nothing. Operand is the three populations, so no store. -partial is the case that earns its place: 'ship' is declared in smFrom and smTrigger but has no smTo row, and it must DROP. The join is inner. An outer join would emit an edge with a hole in it, and a half-declared transition would become a real machine edge leading nowhere -- passing -two and -notrig unchanged while doing it.",
DEF("case:sm-join-two", S2(A("system:sm_join"),
    S3(S2(S2(A("pay"), A("In Cart")), S2(A("ship"), A("Paid"))),
       S2(S2(A("pay"), A("E1")), S2(A("ship"), A("E2"))),
       S2(S2(A("pay"), A("Paid")), S2(A("ship"), A("Shipped")))))),
DEF("case:sm-join-partial", S2(A("system:sm_join"),
    S3(S2(S2(A("pay"), A("In Cart")), S2(A("ship"), A("Paid"))),
       S2(S2(A("pay"), A("E1")), S2(A("ship"), A("E2"))),
       S1(S2(A("pay"), A("Paid")))))),
DEF("case:sm-join-notrig", S2(A("system:sm_join"),
    S3(S1(S2(A("pay"), A("In Cart"))), PHI(), S1(S2(A("pay"), A("Paid")))))),

"constraints:scoped_inclusive_or_spec and scoped_exclusive_or_spec, the absorbed routes for the two disjunctive constraints. Host-called (engine.py 765, 790) and reached by nothing. Operand is <subject_cell, triples> -- the subject stays a NAMED CELL while the clauses become <target, clause_id, popspec> triples, which is the asymmetry to notice and is stated at engine.py:757. Both answer exactly what their plain forms answer: c alone for the inclusive form (in no clause), and c THEN b for the exclusive one (in none, and in both), none-arm before both-arm. These took no failed attempts, unlike scoped_exclusion_spec directly above, for one reason -- the caller was read before the operand was written.",
DEF("case:inclusive-or-spec", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("constraints:scoped_inclusive_or_spec")),
                K(S2(A("Base"), S2(S3(A("P"), A("P"), S2(A("cell"), A("P"))),
                                    S3(A("P"), A("Q"), S2(A("cell"), A("Q")))))))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b"))),
       S3(S3(A("CELL"), A("Base"), S3(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b")), S1(A("c")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("P"), S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("Q"), S1(S1(A("b")))))))),
DEF("case:exclusive-or-spec-mixed", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("constraints:scoped_exclusive_or_spec")),
                K(S2(A("Base"), S2(S3(A("P"), A("P"), S2(A("cell"), A("P"))),
                                    S3(A("P"), A("Q"), S2(A("cell"), A("Q")))))))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b"))),
       S3(S3(A("CELL"), A("Base"), S3(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b")), S1(A("c")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("P"), S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("Q"), S1(S1(A("b")))))))),
DEF("case:exclusive-or-spec-clean", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("constraints:scoped_exclusive_or_spec")),
                K(S2(A("Base"), S2(S3(A("P"), A("P"), S2(A("cell"), A("P"))),
                                    S3(A("P"), A("Q"), S2(A("cell"), A("Q")))))))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S1(S1(A("a"))),
       S3(S3(A("CELL"), A("Base"), S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("P"), S1(S1(A("a")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("Q"), S1(S1(A("b")))))))),

"constraints:scoped_exclusion_spec is the ABSORBED clause's exclusion, and its operand is NOT the plain form's. engine.py:730 marshals <target, clause_id, popspec> TRIPLES, popspec being <'cell', clause> or <'view', table, col>; the plain form takes <<ftA,ftB>, ft>. The two DEFs differ only by participation_spec versus participation -- one ALPHA over the triples, the other a distl of the ft across the clauses -- which is exactly the kind of difference that is invisible in the DEF text and decisive at the call. I guessed four operand shapes and got bottom four times before reading the marshaller. The answers then match the plain form's byte for byte, which is the property worth pinning: the same violation, reached through the absorbed route.",
DEF("case:exclusion-spec-violates", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("constraints:scoped_exclusion_spec")),
                K(S2(S3(A("P"), A("P"), S2(A("cell"), A("P"))),
                     S3(A("P"), A("Q"), S2(A("cell"), A("Q"))))))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b"))),
       S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("P"), S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("Q"), S2(S1(A("b")), S1(A("c")))))))),
DEF("case:exclusion-spec-clean", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("constraints:scoped_exclusion_spec")),
                K(S2(S3(A("P"), A("P"), S2(A("cell"), A("P"))),
                     S3(A("P"), A("Q"), S2(A("cell"), A("Q"))))))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S1(S1(A("a"))),
       S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("P"), S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("Q"), S2(S1(A("b")), S1(A("c")))))))),

"system:class_rule builds the classification rule a value-literal clause reduces to: compiler.py 1825 hands it <pred_clauses, headlit> where a clause is <ft, predicate-tree> -- the predicate arriving as DATA, an eq tree over the field row -- and nothing reached it. The term applies to the STORE, fetching its clause's population by name, and answers <value, headlit> pairs. -dedup is the arm worth having: the same row twice classifies ONCE, because a classification is a set membership and not a count. An implementation without the dedup answers two identical pairs, which every other case here would accept and which would then inflate every population derived from it.",
DEF("case:class-rule-match", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:class_rule")),
                K(S2(S1(S2(A("F1"), S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), N(2), S2(A("CONST"), A("big")))))), A("H"))))),
          A("id"))),
    S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("F1"),
          S3(S2(A("t1"), A("big")), S2(A("t2"), A("small")), S2(A("t3"), A("big"))))))),
DEF("case:class-rule-none", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:class_rule")),
                K(S2(S1(S2(A("F1"), S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), N(2), S2(A("CONST"), A("big")))))), A("H"))))),
          A("id"))),
    S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("F1"), S1(S2(A("t2"), A("small"))))))),
DEF("case:class-rule-dedup", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:class_rule")),
                K(S2(S1(S2(A("F1"), S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), N(2), S2(A("CONST"), A("big")))))), A("H"))))),
          A("id"))),
    S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("F1"), S2(S2(A("t1"), A("big")), S2(A("t1"), A("big"))))))),

"system:join_rule2 builds the two-hop join a derivation rule reduces to: compiler.py 1766 hands it <2, <1>> -- join A's column 2 to B's column 1, project column 1 -- and nothing reached it. These APPLY the built term to two populations rather than pin its shape. -two-hits is the one that earns its place: both a and b hop through the SAME x, so the answer carries both, and an implementation taking the first match per right-hand row would answer <a> alone and pass -match and -nomatch unchanged. A join that silently drops the second path is how a transitive closure comes back short.",
DEF("case:join-rule2-match", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:join_rule2")), K(S2(N(2), S1(N(1)))))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S2(S2(A("a"), A("x")), S2(A("b"), A("y"))),
       S2(S2(A("x"), A("p")), S2(A("z"), A("q")))))),
DEF("case:join-rule2-nomatch", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:join_rule2")), K(S2(N(2), S1(N(1)))))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S1(S2(A("a"), A("x"))), S1(S2(A("z"), A("q")))))),
DEF("case:join-rule2-two-hits", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:join_rule2")), K(S2(N(2), S1(N(1)))))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S2(S2(A("a"), A("x")), S2(A("b"), A("x"))),
       S1(S2(A("x"), A("p")))))),

"system:ask_pos resolves a noun to its role position within a fact type, <ft, k, D>. Host-called (protocol.py 2757) and uncased. It is the OTHER HALF of ask_filter below and casing both closes the loop: a noun the fact type does not play answers '#', and that '#' is exactly the spec value ask_filter's -hash case must reject. Before these two, neither end of that contract was checked on any host -- the producer could have answered phi or bottom and the consumer could have treated it as no-constraint, and each would have looked correct alone. -order and -size together pin that the position is the ROLE's, not a fixed column: the same store answers 1 and 2 for its two players.",
DEF("case:ask-pos-size", S2(A("system:ask_pos"),
    S3(A("Order_has_Size"), A("Size"),
       S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("role"),
             S2(S4(A("Order_has_Size.1"), A("Order_has_Size"), N(1), A("Order")),
                S4(A("Order_has_Size.2"), A("Order_has_Size"), N(2), A("Size")))))))),
DEF("case:ask-pos-order", S2(A("system:ask_pos"),
    S3(A("Order_has_Size"), A("Order"),
       S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("role"),
             S2(S4(A("Order_has_Size.1"), A("Order_has_Size"), N(1), A("Order")),
                S4(A("Order_has_Size.2"), A("Order_has_Size"), N(2), A("Size")))))))),
DEF("case:ask-pos-missing", S2(A("system:ask_pos"),
    S3(A("Order_has_Size"), A("Color"),
       S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("role"),
             S2(S4(A("Order_has_Size.1"), A("Order_has_Size"), N(1), A("Order")),
                S4(A("Order_has_Size.2"), A("Order_has_Size"), N(2), A("Size")))))))),

"system:ask_filter: the plan query's row filter, <specs, rows> with specs <pos, value>. Host-called (protocol.py 2761) and uncased, and it needs no store because ask_pos has already resolved each noun to a position by then. -hash is the case that matters and it is a SAFETY property: protocol.py 2732 says a missing noun answers the # from system:ask_pos, so a spec can arrive with # where a position belongs, and the filter must then match NOTHING. An implementation that treated an unresolvable position as no-constraint would return the WHOLE population for a query filtering on a noun that does not exist -- passing -match and -none unchanged while silently answering every row in the store.",
DEF("case:ask-filter-match", S2(A("system:ask_filter"),
    S2(S1(S2(N(2), A("big"))),
       S3(S2(A("o1"), A("big")), S2(A("o2"), A("small")), S2(A("o3"), A("big")))))),
DEF("case:ask-filter-none", S2(A("system:ask_filter"),
    S2(S1(S2(N(2), A("huge"))),
       S3(S2(A("o1"), A("big")), S2(A("o2"), A("small")), S2(A("o3"), A("big")))))),
DEF("case:ask-filter-hash", S2(A("system:ask_filter"),
    S2(S1(S2(A("#"), A("big"))),
       S3(S2(A("o1"), A("big")), S2(A("o2"), A("small")), S2(A("o3"), A("big")))))),

"system:nav_of: the rows sharing the HEAD row's key -- HATEOAS navigation off a fact. Uncased and host-called, and the operand is just a population plus a key position (engine.py:960 gives the shape as alpha(1) . Filter(key(f) = headKey) . distr . [id, key.1]), so no store is needed. The key comes from the FIRST row, not from an argument, which is the thing to get wrong: -head-o2 puts a different row first over the same population and the answer changes, so a version that filtered on a fixed key or returned everything fails one of the two. -single is the degenerate arm, a population that is all one key.",
DEF("case:nav-of-head-o1", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:nav_of")), K(N(1)))),
          A("id"))),
    S3(S2(A("o1"), A("a")), S2(A("o2"), A("b")), S2(A("o1"), A("c"))))),
DEF("case:nav-of-head-o2", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:nav_of")), K(N(1)))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S2(A("o2"), A("b")), S2(A("o1"), A("a"))))),
DEF("case:nav-of-single", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:nav_of")), K(N(1)))),
          A("id"))),
    S1(S2(A("o1"), A("a"))))),

"system:row_resolve: the entity-cell write. Host-called (rp_row_resolve, main.rs 9247) and reached by nothing. Builder takes <col, width, unary?> and the term applies to <I, row> where I is <key, value> -- engine.py:2590 -- so no store is needed. The three arms are the whole contract and the third is the one worth having: writing into a HOLE fills it, writing the SAME value again is idempotent, and a CONFLICTING functional write makes the column bottom, which by Backus 13.2 collapses the whole row -- so the case REFUSES, and refusing identically on four hosts is the claim. engine.py's own docstring says the step's transition rule then refuses the write atomically: absorption is what makes the uniqueness constraint structural rather than checked.",
DEF("case:row-resolve-hole", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:row_resolve")), K(S3(N(2), N(3), A("F"))))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S2(A("o1"), A("x")), S3(A("o1"), A("#"), A("#"))))),
DEF("case:row-resolve-idempotent", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:row_resolve")), K(S3(N(2), N(3), A("F"))))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S2(A("o1"), A("x")), S3(A("o1"), A("x"), A("#"))))),
DEF("case:row-resolve-conflict", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:row_resolve")), K(S3(N(2), N(3), A("F"))))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S2(A("o1"), A("y")), S3(A("o1"), A("x"), A("#"))))),

"system:transitions_of: the transitions available from the head fact's status. Host-called (rp_transitions_of, main.rs 9239) and reached by nothing. The builder takes <sm, status_pos> where sm is a plain value <<from, trigger, to>...> -- engine.py:966 says so, which is why this one needs no compiled store -- and the TERM it answers is applied to the rows. These APPLY it rather than pin its shape. -terminal is the case that earns its place: a status with no outgoing transition answers phi, and an implementation that skipped the from = status filter would return the whole machine for every status and still pass the other two.",
DEF("case:transitions-of-first", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:transitions_of")),
                K(S2(S2(S3(A("In Cart"), A("pay"), A("Paid")),
                        S3(A("Paid"), A("ship"), A("Shipped"))), N(2))))),
          A("id"))),
    S1(S2(A("o1"), A("In Cart"))))),
DEF("case:transitions-of-mid", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:transitions_of")),
                K(S2(S2(S3(A("In Cart"), A("pay"), A("Paid")),
                        S3(A("Paid"), A("ship"), A("Shipped"))), N(2))))),
          A("id"))),
    S1(S2(A("o1"), A("Paid"))))),
DEF("case:transitions-of-terminal", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:transitions_of")),
                K(S2(S2(S3(A("In Cart"), A("pay"), A("Paid")),
                        S3(A("Paid"), A("ship"), A("Shipped"))), N(2))))),
          A("id"))),
    S1(S2(A("o1"), A("Shipped"))))),

"system:governed_player: which player of a fact type has a noun some machine governs. Host-called (mf_governed_player, main.rs 7377) and reached by nothing -- and it could not be cased until now, because it reads the smDef population and NOTHING EVER WROTE ONE. The grammar rule that classifies a State Machine Reading named the wrong predicate, so sm_def never dispatched; with that fixed the store carries smDef and this answers. The operand is <ft, D> and the store here is 213 characters, hand-built from what a real compile writes: smDef plus the role rows the join needs. -none is the discriminating half: a fact type whose players no machine governs answers phi, and an implementation that returned the role-1 player unconditionally would pass the first case and fail this one.",
DEF("case:governed-player", S2(A("system:governed_player"),
    S2(A("Order_is_paid_by_Customer"),
       S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("smDef"), S1(S2(A("Order"), A("Order")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("role"),
             S2(S4(A("Order_is_paid_by_Customer.1"), A("Order_is_paid_by_Customer"), N(1), A("Order")),
                S4(A("Order_is_paid_by_Customer.2"), A("Order_is_paid_by_Customer"), N(2), A("Customer")))))))),
DEF("case:governed-player-none", S2(A("system:governed_player"),
    S2(A("Customer"),
       S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("smDef"), S1(S2(A("Order"), A("Order")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("role"),
             S2(S4(A("Order_is_paid_by_Customer.1"), A("Order_is_paid_by_Customer"), N(1), A("Order")),
                S4(A("Order_is_paid_by_Customer.2"), A("Order_is_paid_by_Customer"), N(2), A("Customer")))))))),

"system:pass_order and system:pass_bound are CONSTANTS -- K terms, ignoring their operand -- and scheduler_cells_native evaluates both to materialise passOrder and passBound. Constants are the easiest thing to leave uncased and the cheapest thing to get wrong: if one host's canon carried a different literal, that host would schedule the joint loop differently and nothing else in the repo would say so. THE ASYMMETRY IS THE REASON TO CASE THEM RATHER THAN ASSUME. pass_order names FOUR passes -- agg, keyed, sweep, dred -- while system:classify_heads has FIVE arms, the fifth being aggwhole, and scheduler_cells_native emits passHeads rows for all five. So aggwhole heads are classified and materialised but carry no order position. That is either deliberate (aggwhole is dispatched elsewhere, not by the ordered loop) or a gap; the case pins WHAT IS, so that whichever it is stops being invisible. pass_bound is the round cap, 12.",
DEF("case:pass-order", S2(A("system:pass_order"), PHI())),
DEF("case:pass-bound", S2(A("system:pass_bound"), PHI())),

"constraints:scoped_mandatory_entities_implied closes the scoped_* cluster. Host-called at main.rs 13117, reached by nothing. The spec is <pos, rows> and each row is <kind, name, vcol, col> with kind one of P, view or cell -- read off main.rs 13096-13108, NOT inferred from the DEF, which only shows that impl_member selects position 4. That distinction is the working method of this whole cluster: the builder tells you what it composes, the call site tells you what it is handed, and only the second can be guessed wrong in a way four green hosts would ratify. Applied to <rows, D> the answer is the implied entities MISSING from the population: c is in F and not in the rows, so <c>; -holds has every implied entity present and answers phi.",
DEF("case:mandatory-implied-holds", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("constraints:scoped_mandatory_entities_implied")),
                K(S2(N(1), S1(S4(A("cell"), A("F"), N(0), N(1))))))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("c"))),
       S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("F"), S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("c")))))))),
DEF("case:mandatory-implied", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("constraints:scoped_mandatory_entities_implied")),
                K(S2(N(1), S1(S4(A("cell"), A("F"), N(0), N(1))))))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b"))),
       S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("F"), S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("c")))))))),

"constraints:scoped_equality_side, host-called at compile.rs 2216 with a BARE ft name, reached by nothing. It is a SYMMETRIC DIFFERENCE and that is the whole content: cat over <setminus<rows,pop>, setminus<pop,rows>>, so an equality constraint reports the strays on BOTH sides rather than one. -right-only is the case that cannot be faked: rows are a subset of the population, so the first setminus is empty and ONLY the second arm fires. A one-directional implementation -- the obvious way to write this, and the way scoped_subset above is legitimately written -- answers phi there and passes -both and -equal unchanged. -both also pins the ORDER: the rows-side strays precede the population-side ones, which a set-wise comparison would not notice.",
DEF("case:equality-side-both", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("constraints:scoped_equality_side")), K(A("F")))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b"))),
       S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("F"), S2(S1(A("b")), S1(A("c")))))))),
DEF("case:equality-side-right-only", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("constraints:scoped_equality_side")), K(A("F")))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S1(S1(A("b"))),
       S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("F"), S2(S1(A("b")), S1(A("c")))))))),
DEF("case:equality-side-equal", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("constraints:scoped_equality_side")), K(A("F")))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b"))),
       S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("F"), S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b")))))))),

"constraints:scoped_mandatory_facts, host-called at compile.rs 828 and 856 and reached by nothing. Note the operand is a BARE ft name, not a sequence -- vs(ft), not vt(...) -- which is the first thing to get wrong here and the reason these were written from the call site rather than from the DEF. Applied to <rows, D> it is setminus over <proj1 of rows, proj1 of pop_of(ft)>: the entities that fail to participate. Both sides are projected to column 1, so an entity is matched by its ID and not by its whole row -- -wide gives the rows a second column the population does not have, and the answer must still be phi. Without that case an implementation comparing whole rows passes -violates and -holds and fails only on real data.",
DEF("case:mandatory-facts-violates", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("constraints:scoped_mandatory_facts")), K(A("F")))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S3(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b")), S1(A("c"))),
       S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("F"), S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b")))))))),
DEF("case:mandatory-facts-holds", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("constraints:scoped_mandatory_facts")), K(A("F")))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b"))),
       S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("F"), S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b")))))))),
DEF("case:mandatory-facts-wide", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("constraints:scoped_mandatory_facts")), K(A("F")))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S2(S2(A("a"), A("x")), S2(A("b"), A("y"))),
       S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("F"), S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b")))))))),

"constraints:scoped_exclusive_or, operand <base, clauses, ft> as compile.rs 2197 passes it -- the same shape as the inclusive form, and the difference is the whole point. EXCLUSIVE-OR FAILS IN TWO DIRECTIONS and one operand exposes both: -mixed puts a in P alone (fine), b in BOTH P and Q, and c in NEITHER, and the answer is <c> then <b>. That order is the answer's own and is pinned deliberately: the none-arm is concatenated before the both-arm, so a version that computed the right two sets and emitted them the other way round is a difference the unit tests would show. -clean has every member in exactly one clause and answers phi; without it, an implementation that reported every base member would still pass -mixed, since -mixed's answer happens to omit only a.",
DEF("case:scoped-exclusive-or-clean", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("constraints:scoped_exclusive_or")),
                K(S3(A("Base"), S2(A("P"), A("Q")), A("P"))))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S1(S1(A("a"))),
       S3(S3(A("CELL"), A("Base"), S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("P"), S1(S1(A("a")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("Q"), S1(S1(A("b")))))))),
DEF("case:scoped-exclusive-or-mixed", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("constraints:scoped_exclusive_or")),
                K(S3(A("Base"), S2(A("P"), A("Q")), A("P"))))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b"))),
       S3(S3(A("CELL"), A("Base"), S3(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b")), S1(A("c")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("P"), S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("Q"), S1(S1(A("b")))))))),

"constraints:scoped_inclusive_or, operand <base, clauses, ft> as compile.rs 2204 passes it. Violations are the base members participating in NO clause. THE PAIR IS THE POINT and neither case alone would show it: the FIRST element of the applied operand -- the scoped population -- PARTICIPATES, it is not merely the thing being checked. With rows <a,b> only c violates; with rows phi the answer grows to <a,c>, because a is then covered by nothing. I found that by running the phi version first, predicting <c>, and getting <a,c>: the surprise was mine, and the second case exists to pin the mechanism rather than to leave the first looking arbitrary. An implementation that read participation only out of D would answer <a,c> for both.",
DEF("case:scoped-inclusive-or-violates", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("constraints:scoped_inclusive_or")),
                K(S3(A("Base"), S2(A("P"), A("Q")), A("P"))))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b"))),
       S3(S3(A("CELL"), A("Base"), S3(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b")), S1(A("c")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("P"), S1(S1(A("a")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("Q"), S1(S1(A("b")))))))),
DEF("case:scoped-inclusive-or-empty-scope", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("constraints:scoped_inclusive_or")),
                K(S3(A("Base"), S2(A("P"), A("Q")), A("P"))))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(PHI(),
       S3(S3(A("CELL"), A("Base"), S3(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b")), S1(A("c")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("P"), S1(S1(A("a")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("Q"), S1(S1(A("b")))))))),

"constraints:scoped_exclusion, applied the same way as scoped_subset above -- operand <<ftA,ftB>, ft> as compile.rs 1379 passes it, term applied to <rows, D>. The answer NAMES WHERE the offender participated: b in both P and Q comes back as <b,P> and <b,Q>, which is more than a violation flag and is what makes the -same-pop case worth having. That one puts b in P TWICE and nowhere else: exclusion is about participating in DIFFERENT populations, not about repeating within one, so it must answer phi. An implementation that reduced exclusion to plain uniqueness over the concatenated rows would report a violation there, and would look correct on every other case here.",
DEF("case:scoped-exclusion-violates", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("constraints:scoped_exclusion")),
                K(S2(S2(A("P"), A("Q")), A("P"))))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b"))),
       S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("P"), S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("Q"), S2(S1(A("b")), S1(A("c")))))))),

DEF("case:scoped-exclusion-holds", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("constraints:scoped_exclusion")),
                K(S2(S2(A("P"), A("Q")), A("P"))))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b"))),
       S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("P"), S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("Q"), S1(S1(A("c")))))))),
DEF("case:scoped-exclusion-same-pop", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("constraints:scoped_exclusion")),
                K(S2(S2(A("P"), A("Q")), A("P"))))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S1(S1(A("b"))),
       S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("P"), S2(S1(A("b")), S1(A("b")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("Q"), PHI()))))),

"constraints:scoped_subset is host-called (compile.rs 1205, 2212) and was reached by nothing. Unlike the compile_rule cases above, these APPLY the built term instead of pinning its shape: the term is setminus over <population, pop_of(sup) of D>, so a two-element operand <rows, D> is all it needs and the answer is the VIOLATION set. That is the stronger check -- a host that builds the right term and reduces it wrongly fails here, which the shape cases cannot see. -violates puts c in the subtype and not in Person, so the answer is <<c>>: exactly the members that break the subset. -holds has every member present and answers phi, and it is the case a broken implementation passes by accident if it answers phi for everything, which is why the violating case leads. -empty-super means nothing is a Person, so EVERY subtype member violates -- the arm where a fetch that answered phi-as-success rather than phi-as-empty would silently report no violations at all.",
DEF("case:scoped-subset-violates", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("constraints:scoped_subset")), K(A("Person")))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("c"))),
       S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("Person"), S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b")))))))),
DEF("case:scoped-subset-holds", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("constraints:scoped_subset")), K(A("Person")))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S1(S1(A("a"))),
       S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("Person"), S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b")))))))),
DEF("case:scoped-subset-empty-super", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("constraints:scoped_subset")), K(A("Person")))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("c"))), PHI()))),

"system:compile_rule and its delta and neg variants build the TERM a rule body reduces to, and compile.rs names all three (1210, 1991, 2025) while no case or law reached any of them -- the closure's correctness rests on these and all four hosts could have built different terms silently. The operand is <atoms, projection, negations> exactly as compile.rs passes it. What these pin is the BUILT TERM, byte for byte, not its behavior when applied: a host that compiled the same rule into a different shape is caught, a host that compiled the right shape and reduced it wrongly is not. That is a real limit and the reason to say it here rather than let the green be read as more than it is -- applying the term needs a store, which is derive:eval's territory and a different case. The three arities are NOT the same and assuming they were is how this started: plain takes <atoms, proj, negs>, delta takes a FOURTH element naming which atom becomes the ~d variant, and neg takes five <aspecs, proj, cols, filters, negspecs>. The first attempt fed all three the plain shape and two REFUSED, which is the case table doing its job before the note was written. -delta-1of2 and -2of2 exist because the single-atom delta case is degenerate -- with one atom the index cannot discriminate, and the two-atom pair answers two DIFFERENT terms of the same length, so the index is shown to select rather than assumed to.",
DEF("case:compile-rule", S2(A("system:compile_rule"),
    S3(S1(S3(A("F1"), N(1), PHI())), S1(N(1)), PHI()))),
DEF("case:compile-rule-delta", S2(A("system:compile_rule_delta"),
    S4(S1(S3(A("F1"), N(1), PHI())), S1(N(1)), PHI(), N(1)))),
DEF("case:compile-rule-delta-1of2", S2(A("system:compile_rule_delta"),
    S4(S2(S3(A("F1"), N(1), PHI()), S3(A("F2"), N(1), PHI())),
       S1(N(1)), PHI(), N(1)))),
DEF("case:compile-rule-delta-2of2", S2(A("system:compile_rule_delta"),
    S4(S2(S3(A("F1"), N(1), PHI()), S3(A("F2"), N(1), PHI())),
       S1(N(1)), PHI(), N(2)))),
DEF("case:compile-rule-neg", S2(A("system:compile_rule_neg"),
    S5(S1(S3(A("F1"), N(1), PHI())), S1(N(1)), S1(N(1)), PHI(), PHI()))),

"rmap:keyof and rmap:ddl_ref were BOTH host-called and reachable from no case -- engine/rust names them at main.rs 10937 and 11117 and nothing held any host to either. keyof is refmode composed with keycol, and refmode's own three cases already pin which cell wins; what these add is that the composition threads the SAME operand into both halves, so -scheme-last must answer person_nr and not person_ignored. The -default case is the one a wrong composition passes anyway, which is why the scheme case leads. ddl_ref is the REFERENCES clause: both identifiers QUOTED, the parens tight around the column, and the leading space belongs to the clause because the caller concatenates it onto a column spec -- dropping it yields a syntactically valid string that produces invalid SQL, the failure a case that only looked for the word REFERENCES would miss. The -spaced case pins that ddl_ref QUOTES and does not SLUG: engine/rust slugs the parent through ddl_sql_name BEFORE calling (main.rs 11190), so a name with a space reaching this DEF comes back quoted verbatim rather than folded to order_line. That division is the contract -- ddl_ref is not the naming step -- and casing it stops a later reader from 'fixing' it by adding rmap:ddl_name here, which would slug an already-slugged name.",
DEF("case:keyof-scheme-last", S2(A("rmap:keyof"),
    S3(A("Person"),
       S2(S2(A("Person"), A("code")), S2(A("Person"), A("nr"))),
       S1(S2(A("Person"), A("ignored")))))),
DEF("case:keyof-default", S2(A("rmap:keyof"), S3(A("Van"), PHI(), PHI()))),
DEF("case:ddl-ref", S2(A("rmap:ddl_ref"), S2(A("Person"), A("person_nr")))),
DEF("case:ddl-ref-spaced", S2(A("rmap:ddl_ref"), S2(A("Order Line"), A("order_nr")))),
DEF("case:rolegroups", S2(A("rmap:rolegroups"),
    S3(S4(A("r3"), A("F1"), N(2), A("Widget")),
       S4(A("r9"), A("F2"), N(1), A("Other")),
       S4(A("r1"), A("F1"), N(1), A("Thing"))))),
"rolesof's guards, each with a row that would previously have answered bottom for the WHOLE grouping rather than being skipped: a three-wide row (no player to read at 4) and a row at position 0. Both sit between two good rows, so a fold that bottomed, or that stopped at the first bad row, loses the good row after them too.",
"rmap:role_maxpos. The -wide3 case is the point: a THREE-wide row must COUNT, where rolesof's >= 4 guard would drop it -- only the fact type and position are read here. The -none case pins the default of 2, not 0: the caller asks whether the maximum is 1 to decide unary, so a missing fact type answering 0 would invert that test. The two-wide row is skipped, and another fact type's higher position must not leak in.",
DEF("case:role-maxpos", S2(A("rmap:role_maxpos"),
    S2(A("F1"),
       S3(S4(A("r1"), A("F1"), N(1), A("Thing")),
          S4(A("r2"), A("F2"), N(9), A("Other")),
          S4(A("r3"), A("F1"), N(3), A("Widget")))))),
DEF("case:role-maxpos-wide3", S2(A("rmap:role_maxpos"),
    S2(A("F1"),
       S2(S3(A("r1"), A("F1"), N(4)), S2(A("r2"), A("F1")))))),
DEF("case:role-maxpos-none", S2(A("rmap:role_maxpos"),
    S2(A("F9"),
       S1(S4(A("r1"), A("F1"), N(1), A("Thing")))))),
DEF("case:rolesof-guards", S2(A("rmap:rolesof"),
    S2(A("F1"),
       S4(S4(A("r1"), A("F1"), N(1), A("Thing")),
          S3(A("r2"), A("F1"), N(2)),
          S4(A("r3"), A("F1"), N(0), A("Zero")),
          S4(A("r4"), A("F1"), N(3), A("Widget")))))),
DEF("case:rolesof", S2(A("rmap:rolesof"),
    S2(A("F1"),
       S3(S4(A("r3"), A("F1"), N(2), A("Widget")),
          S4(A("r9"), A("F2"), N(1), A("Other")),
          S4(A("r1"), A("F1"), N(1), A("Thing")))))),
DEF("case:rolesof-tie", S2(A("rmap:rolesof"),
    S2(A("F1"),
       S2(S4(A("r1"), A("F1"), N(1), A("Beta")),
          S4(A("r2"), A("F1"), N(1), A("Alpha")))))),
DEF("case:colsof", S2(A("rmap:colsof"),
    S2(A("person"),
       S3(S3(A("person"), N(3), A("ftC")),
          S3(A("other"), N(2), A("ftX")),
          S3(A("person"), N(2), A("ftA")))))),
DEF("case:coldisamb", S2(A("rmap:coldisamb"),
    S5(A("a"), A("b"), A("a"), A("a"), A("c")))),
DEF("case:ddl-order", S2(A("rmap:ddl_order"),
    S4(S2(A("child"), S1(A("parent"))),
       S2(A("parent"), PHI()),
       S2(A("orphan"), S1(A("external"))),
       S2(A("self"), S1(A("self")))))),
DEF("case:ddl-order-cycle", S2(A("rmap:ddl_order"),
    S2(S2(A("a"), S1(A("b"))),
       S2(A("b"), S1(A("a")))))),
DEF("case:strdown", S2(A("strdown"), A("AbC"))),
DEF("case:ddlname-plain", S2(A("rmap:ddl_name"), A("Person Name"))),
DEF("case:ddlname-guard", S2(A("rmap:ddl_name"), A("SQLite Fact Base"))),
DEF("case:rmap-ddl-entity", S2(A("rmap:ddl_table"),
    S3(A("person"),
       S2(S3(A("person_id"), A("TEXT PRIMARY KEY"), A("")),
          S3(A("is_active"), A("BOOLEAN"), A(""))),
       PHI()))),
DEF("case:rmap-ddl-relation", S2(A("rmap:ddl_table"),
    S3(A("likes"),
       S2(S3(A("person_id"), A("TEXT"), A(" REFERENCES \"person\"(\"person_id\")")),
          S3(A("thing_id"), A("TEXT"), A(""))),
       S2(A("person_id"), A("thing_id"))))),
DEF("case:rmap-wide-row-mixed", S2(A("rmap:wide_row"),
    S2(A("a"),
       S2(S5(A("F1"), S2(A("Thing"), A("Widget")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(),
             S1(S2(A("a"), A("x")))),
          S5(A("F2"), S2(A("Thing"), A("Gadget")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(),
             S1(S2(A("b"), A("y")))))))),
DEF("case:vb-fetch-absorbed", S2(A("system:vb_fetch"), S2(A("P_has_N"), S3(S3(A("CELL"), A("rmapColumns"), S1(S3(A("P"), N(2), A("P_has_N")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("P"), S1(S1(A("p1")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("P:p1"), S2(A("p1"), A("Ada"))))))),
DEF("case:vb-fetch-total", S2(A("system:vb_fetch"), S2(A("Nope"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("x"), S1(S1(A("y")))))))),
DEF("case:dynfetch", S2(A("ast:DynFetch"), S2(A("P:p1"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("P:p1"), S2(A("p1"), A("Ada"))))))),
DEF("case:vb-colrow", S2(A("system:vb_colrow"), S2(A("P_has_N"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("rmapColumns"), S1(S3(A("P"), N(2), A("P_has_N")))))))),
DEF("case:ftpop-absorbed-mini", S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"), S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:ftpop_absorbed")), K(S2(A("P"), N(2))))), A("id"))), S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("P"), S1(S1(A("p1")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("P:p1"), S2(A("p1"), A("Ada")))))),
DEF("case:sel-str", S2(A("2"), S3(A("x"), A("y"), A("z")))),
DEF("case:ftpop-fetch-mini", S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"), S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:ftpop_fetch")), K(S2(A("P"), N(2))))), A("id"))), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("P"), S1(S1(A("p1"))))))),
DEF("case:ftpop-pairsel-mini", S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"), S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:ftpop_pairsel")), K(S2(A("P"), N(2))))), A("id"))), S2(S1(A("p1")), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("P:p1"), S2(A("p1"), A("Ada"))))))),

DEF("case:lex-words", S2(A("lex"), A("Each Task1 occurs."))),
DEF("case:lex-quoted", S2(A("lex"), A("Status 'In Cart' is to Status 'Placed'."))),
DEF("case:lex-hyphen", S2(A("lex"), A("Layer has valence- Coord and Type -adj and FORE-- WORD and from-Status"))),
DEF("case:implode-tpl", S2(A("implode"), S2(A(" "), S3(A("{0}"), A("was"), A("{1}"))))),
DEF("case:implode-us", S2(A("implode"), S2(A("_"), S2(A("a"), A("b"))))),
DEF("case:slug-reading", S2(A("slug"), A("Order was placed by Customer"))),
DEF("case:slug-hyphen", S2(A("slug"), A("place-receipt"))),

DEF("case:reading-parse", S2(A("system:reading_parse"), S3(A("Order was placed by Customer"), S2(A("Order"), A("Customer")), S2(A("The"), A("Each"))))),
DEF("case:reading-parse-munch", S2(A("system:reading_parse"), S3(A("Event Type has Name"), S3(A("Event"), A("Event Type"), A("Name")), S2(A("The"), A("Each"))))),

"system:con_text both ways round: the two sentences that need a second player and the two guards that must answer NOTHING rather than a half-built sentence. con-text-deontic is spelled deonticObligation on purpose -- the arm tests a PREFIX, so a case using the bare word deontic would pass just as well against an equality test and would not pin the behavior the generator has.",
DEF("case:con-text-uc", S2(A("system:con_text"), S3(A("uniqueness"), A("c1"), S2(A("Person"), A("Name"))))),
DEF("case:con-text-mc", S2(A("system:con_text"), S3(A("mandatory"), A("c2"), S2(A("Person"), A("Name"))))),
DEF("case:con-text-deontic", S2(A("system:con_text"), S3(A("deonticObligation"), A("c4"), S2(A("A"), A("B"))))),
DEF("case:con-text-unary", S2(A("system:con_text"), S3(A("uniqueness"), A("c5"), S1(A("Person"))))),
DEF("case:con-text-other", S2(A("system:con_text"), S3(A("other"), A("c6"), S2(A("A"), A("B"))))),

"system:con_pair answers <tag, sentence> -- what the generator files, not just what it reads. con-pair-deontic is the row that looks wrong and is not: a deontic constraint files under UC, the same tag as uniqueness, because the tag says how the generator GROUPS the sentence rather than what the constraint is. con-pair-uc and con-pair-mc carry the same two players and differ only in kind, so the pair of them shows the tag and the sentence moving together. The def composes system:con_text rather than restating the sentences, so the tagged and untagged forms cannot drift.",
DEF("case:con-pair-uc", S2(A("system:con_pair"), S3(A("uniqueness"), A("c1"), S2(A("Person"), A("Name"))))),
DEF("case:con-pair-mc", S2(A("system:con_pair"), S3(A("mandatory"), A("c2"), S2(A("Person"), A("Name"))))),
DEF("case:con-pair-deontic", S2(A("system:con_pair"), S3(A("deonticObligation"), A("c4"), S2(A("A"), A("B"))))),

"system:dsl_row with all three components handed over OUT OF ORDER, and the transitions carrying a duplicate. Every component comes back sorted and the duplicate is gone, which is the point: engine.py own comment records that store emission order once leaked into these row bytes and differed across hosts on rule-chain apps. The three sorts are not interchangeable -- readings are flat names, constraints are <tag, sentence> pairs, transitions are <trigger, from, to> triples -- so a case with everything already in order would pass against a def that sorted none of them.",
DEF("case:dsl-row", S2(A("system:dsl_row"), S2(S2(A("Task"), A("entity")), S3(S2(A("b reading"), A("a reading")), S2(S2(A("UC"), A("z sentence")), S2(A("MC"), A("a sentence"))), S3(S3(A("t2"), A("f"), A("x")), S3(A("t1"), A("f"), A("y")), S3(A("t1"), A("f"), A("y"))))))),

"system:ot_kinds over a store carrying a THIRD kind. Odd is an instance of SomethingElse and does not appear: only object types and value types are declared types, and the def inherits that from system:ev_entities and system:ctx_vals rather than re-deciding it. Task comes back after Alpha though it is written first, so the name order is the def and not the store order. The labels are the generator vocabulary -- entity and value where instanceOf says ObjectType and ValueType -- which is the translation this def exists to hold.",
DEF("case:ot-kinds", S2(A("system:ot_kinds"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("instanceOf"), S4(S2(A("Task"), A("ObjectType")), S2(A("Name"), A("ValueType")), S2(A("Odd"), A("SomethingElse")), S2(A("Alpha"), A("ObjectType"))))))),

"constraints:scoped_subset_projected_cfiltered applied to the epistemics shape and run over two rows. s_good is grounded in an AUTHORITATIVE source and must NOT be a violation; s_bad is grounded in an unauthoritative one and must be. That pair is the whole point: the sibling builder filters the CONDITION and would answer the opposite -- violations for the rows that satisfy the rule and none for the row that breaks it -- so a case with only one row would pass against either. The def is built and applied as a constant spec, the shape the ftpop cases use, which is known to reduce on all four hosts.",
DEF("case:subset-cfiltered", S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"), S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("constraints:scoped_subset_projected_cfiltered")), K(S5(A("Source_has_Authority"), S1(N(2)), S1(N(1)), N(2), A("authoritative"))))), A("id"))), S2(S2(S2(A("c1"), A("s_good")), S2(A("c2"), A("s_bad"))), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("Source_has_Authority"), S2(S2(A("s_good"), A("authoritative")), S2(A("s_bad"), A("unauthoritative")))))))),

"system:machine is the tau-ordered fold (INSERT system:mstep over the ordered events) and law:machine already certifies it, but only as a law: the case table compares per case, so this pins the SAME literal row by row across the hosts. The events are deliberately out of order and e9 fires from nowhere -- it is the row proving an event invalid from the current status leaves the status alone rather than bottoming the fold.",
DEF("case:machine-fold", S2(A("system:machine"), S2(S2(A("a"), S2(S4(A("a"), A("t1"), A("b"), A("e1")), S4(A("b"), A("t2"), A("c"), A("e2")))), S3(S2(A("e2"), N(10)), S2(A("e9"), N(2)), S2(A("e1"), N(9)))))),

"system:mgreedy is machine_fold walk, and these four pin what makes it NOT system:machine. mgreedy-fold is case:machine-fold operand exactly, where both agree on c, so it fixes the shared meaning. mgreedy-order is the pair that separates them: the timestamps contradict arrival order, so the tau-ordered fold answers b and the greedy walk answers c, and without this case the two defs would be indistinguishable from the table. mgreedy-repeat fires the same event type twice around a b to a loop and answers a, which is the POSITIONAL removal: consuming by value would drop both rows on the first firing and stop at b. mgreedy-none is the no-op, every event invalid from every reachable status, answering the initial status rather than bottom.",
DEF("case:mgreedy-fold", S2(A("system:mgreedy"), S2(S2(A("a"), S2(S4(A("a"), A("t1"), A("b"), A("e1")), S4(A("b"), A("t2"), A("c"), A("e2")))), S3(S2(A("e2"), N(10)), S2(A("e9"), N(2)), S2(A("e1"), N(9)))))),
DEF("case:mgreedy-order", S2(A("system:mgreedy"), S2(S2(A("a"), S2(S4(A("a"), A("t1"), A("b"), A("e1")), S4(A("b"), A("t2"), A("c"), A("e2")))), S2(S2(A("e1"), N(10)), S2(A("e2"), N(1)))))),
DEF("case:mgreedy-repeat", S2(A("system:mgreedy"), S2(S2(A("a"), S2(S4(A("a"), A("t1"), A("b"), A("e1")), S4(A("b"), A("t2"), A("a"), A("e1")))), S2(S2(A("e1"), N(1)), S2(A("e1"), N(2)))))),
DEF("case:mgreedy-none", S2(A("system:mgreedy"), S2(S2(A("a"), S2(S4(A("a"), A("t1"), A("b"), A("e1")), S4(A("b"), A("t2"), A("c"), A("e2")))), S2(S2(A("e9"), N(1)), S2(A("e9"), N(2)))))),

"system:mf_keys both ways. The store is built so the setminus is OBSERVABLE rather than assumed: TaskHasStatus carries t9, a key held nowhere else, so if the status fact type were not excluded from the union t9 would appear in the answer. r3 is role position 2 and r4 heads a different noun, so both are passed over. t1 is reached twice, once from the noun table and once as a role-1 key, and appears once. mf-keys-absent is the noun no cell mentions, answering the empty sequence rather than bottom.",
DEF("case:mf-keys", S2(A("system:mf_keys"), S2(S2(A("Task"), A("TaskHasStatus")), S4(S3(A("CELL"), A("Task"), S2(S1(A("t1")), S1(A("t2")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("role"), S4(S4(A("r1"), A("TaskHasSubject"), N(1), A("Task")), S4(A("r2"), A("TaskHasStatus"), N(1), A("Task")), S4(A("r3"), A("Other"), N(2), A("Task")), S4(A("r4"), A("OtherNoun"), N(1), A("Person")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("TaskHasSubject"), S2(S2(A("t3"), A("x")), S2(A("t1"), A("y")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("TaskHasStatus"), S1(S2(A("t9"), A("open")))))))),
DEF("case:mf-keys-absent", S2(A("system:mf_keys"), S2(S2(A("Nobody"), A("X")), S4(S3(A("CELL"), A("Task"), S2(S1(A("t1")), S1(A("t2")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("role"), S4(S4(A("r1"), A("TaskHasSubject"), N(1), A("Task")), S4(A("r2"), A("TaskHasStatus"), N(1), A("Task")), S4(A("r3"), A("Other"), N(2), A("Task")), S4(A("r4"), A("OtherNoun"), N(1), A("Person")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("TaskHasSubject"), S2(S2(A("t3"), A("x")), S2(A("t1"), A("y")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("TaskHasStatus"), S1(S2(A("t9"), A("open")))))))),

"system:mf_entity, and the last two rows are the pair that pins WHICH comparison the write rule makes. mf-entity-roundtrip walks a to b and back to a with a current of a: it FIRED, but the status equals the current, so it does not write. mf-entity-roundtrip-init is the same walk with no current at all, and it DOES write, because a is not an absent current -- that is SM init landing a status that was missing, and it is why the rule compares against current rather than against the start it chose. mf-entity-quiet fires nothing and stays untouched, mf-entity-init starts from the initial because there is no current, and mf-entity-fires is the ordinary advance.",
DEF("case:mf-entity-fires", S2(A("system:mf_entity"), S2(S3(A("a"), A("a"), S2(S4(A("a"), A("t1"), A("b"), A("e1")), S4(A("b"), A("t2"), A("c"), A("e2")))), S2(S2(A("e1"), N(1)), S2(A("e2"), N(2)))))),
DEF("case:mf-entity-init", S2(A("system:mf_entity"), S2(S3(PHI(), A("a"), S2(S4(A("a"), A("t1"), A("b"), A("e1")), S4(A("b"), A("t2"), A("c"), A("e2")))), S2(S2(A("e1"), N(1)), S2(A("e2"), N(2)))))),
DEF("case:mf-entity-quiet", S2(A("system:mf_entity"), S2(S3(PHI(), A("a"), S2(S4(A("a"), A("t1"), A("b"), A("e1")), S4(A("b"), A("t2"), A("c"), A("e2")))), S1(S2(A("e9"), N(1)))))),
DEF("case:mf-entity-roundtrip", S2(A("system:mf_entity"), S2(S3(A("a"), A("a"), S2(S4(A("a"), A("t1"), A("b"), A("e1")), S4(A("b"), A("t2"), A("a"), A("e1")))), S2(S2(A("e1"), N(1)), S2(A("e1"), N(2)))))),
DEF("case:mf-entity-roundtrip-init", S2(A("system:mf_entity"), S2(S3(PHI(), A("a"), S2(S4(A("a"), A("t1"), A("b"), A("e1")), S4(A("b"), A("t2"), A("a"), A("e1")))), S2(S2(A("e1"), N(1)), S2(A("e1"), N(2)))))),

"system:mf_evkeys reads the event key at the GOVERNED ROLE POSITION, so the position is the variable the cases vary. mf-evkeys-pos1 reads the same rows at position 1 and answers all four keys; mf-evkeys-pos2 reads them at position 2, where two rows carry the empty and phi markers, and answers only the two real ones -- a role left unfilled is not an event for anybody. Reading position 1 unconditionally, which is the tempting simplification, would answer four events for a fact type that has two. mf-evkeys-absent is a fact type no cell holds, answering the empty sequence rather than bottom.",
DEF("case:mf-evkeys-pos1", S2(A("system:mf_evkeys"), S2(S2(A("TaskFinished"), N(1)), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("TaskFinished"), S4(S2(A("t1"), A("x")), S2(A("t2"), A("")), S2(A("t3"), A("φ")), S2(A("t4"), A("y")))))))),
DEF("case:mf-evkeys-pos2", S2(A("system:mf_evkeys"), S2(S2(A("TaskFinished"), N(2)), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("TaskFinished"), S4(S2(A("t1"), A("x")), S2(A("t2"), A("")), S2(A("t3"), A("φ")), S2(A("t4"), A("y")))))))),
DEF("case:mf-evkeys-absent", S2(A("system:mf_evkeys"), S2(S2(A("Nope"), N(1)), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("TaskFinished"), S4(S2(A("t1"), A("x")), S2(A("t2"), A("")), S2(A("t3"), A("φ")), S2(A("t4"), A("y")))))))),

"system:h_finality was one of five compile kinds present in the host dispatch table and absent from canon, which is precisely a kind the thin host cannot compile. The handler is the whole of its compile meaning -- one fact, no objects -- and the cases answer against python _PLAN finality directly, which is the reference implementation sitting beside it. h-finality-zero carries depth 0 because zero is the value a truthiness test would drop, and depth 0 is meaningful: it hardens immediately.",
DEF("case:h-finality", S2(A("system:h_finality"), S3(S2(A("Task"), N(3)), PHI(), A("")))),
DEF("case:h-finality-zero", S2(A("system:h_finality"), S3(S2(A("X"), N(0)), PHI(), A("")))),

"system:subset_proj answers the two projections of an if-then subset. subset-proj is the ordinary crossed binding, where the consequent names the same two nouns in the other order, so the projections differ and neither is the identity -- reading either side positionally would compile a constraint on the wrong roles. subset-proj-dup is the ambiguity: a noun bound TWICE on the antecedent side has no single position to project, so it is dropped rather than resolved to its first occurrence, and both projections come back empty. subset-proj-reorder keeps the antecedent order while the consequent positions jump, which is what fixes the ANSWER ORDER as the antecedent order rather than the consequent one.",
DEF("case:subset-proj", S2(A("system:subset_proj"), S2(S2(A("Message"), A("Rep")), S2(A("Rep"), A("Message"))))),
DEF("case:subset-proj-dup", S2(A("system:subset_proj"), S2(S2(A("M"), A("M")), S1(A("M"))))),
DEF("case:subset-proj-reorder", S2(A("system:subset_proj"), S2(S3(A("A"), A("B"), A("C")), S2(A("C"), A("A"))))),

"system:h_possibility carries the MODALITY, and the two cases are the same sentence under two of them. That is the whole point: possible and necessary are different claims about one reading, so the modality has to come from the handler operand rather than be re-derived from the text, and a handler that dropped it would answer identically for both rows here.",
DEF("case:h-possibility", S2(A("system:h_possibility"), S3(S1(A("some Order is shipped")), PHI(), A("possible")))),
DEF("case:h-possibility-nec", S2(A("system:h_possibility"), S3(S1(A("some Order is shipped")), PHI(), A("necessary")))),

"system:brace_excl answers the marked half of a brace family: ONE exclusion row over the whole family, then ONE partition fact per subtype. brace-excl-three carries three subtypes and a deontic modality, which pins two things a two-member case cannot: the partition rows scale with the family while the exclusion row stays single, and the modality rides through rather than being dropped as ordinary. The exclusion row anchors on the FIRST subtype and carries the whole family beside it, so the anchor and the family are not the same field.",
DEF("case:brace-excl", S2(A("system:brace_excl"), S4(A("sxc_ab"), S2(A("A"), A("B")), A("X"), A("")))),
DEF("case:brace-excl-three", S2(A("system:brace_excl"), S4(A("sxc_pqr"), S3(A("P"), A("Q"), A("R")), A("Party"), A("obligatory")))),

"system:subset_specs binds each scoped cell of an if-then subset to the projected builder. subset-specs-two is the row that matters: TWO cells carrying the SAME operand. The projection is a property of the if-then pair, so recomputing it per cell would let two cells of one constraint disagree about which roles project, and a single-cell case cannot see that.",
DEF("case:subset-specs", S2(A("system:subset_specs"), S2(S1(A("c1")), S3(A("B_ft"), S2(N(1), N(2)), S2(N(2), N(1)))))),
DEF("case:subset-specs-two", S2(A("system:subset_specs"), S2(S2(A("c1"), A("c2")), S3(A("B_ft"), S2(N(1), N(2)), S2(N(2), N(1)))))),

"system:h_dispatch is the compile driver reading system:h_table, the kind-to-handler POPULATION. h-dispatch reaches a handler that ignores modality and h-dispatch-modal one that carries it, so the driver is shown threading the whole operand rather than a convenient part of it. h-dispatch-unknown is the row that keeps the driver honest: a kind no row names answers the EMPTY PAIR, not bottom, which is what lets an unrecognized statement compile to nothing instead of killing the compile -- the host default this replaces did exactly that, and a driver that bottomed instead would fail only on the models nobody has written yet.",
DEF("case:h-dispatch", S2(A("system:h_dispatch"), S4(A("finality"), S2(A("Task"), N(3)), PHI(), A("")))),
DEF("case:h-dispatch-modal", S2(A("system:h_dispatch"), S4(A("possibility"), S1(A("some X")), PHI(), A("necessary")))),
DEF("case:h-dispatch-unknown", S2(A("system:h_dispatch"), S4(A("nosuchkind"), S1(A("a")), PHI(), A("")))),

"system:mf_bysft groups the written statuses by their status fact type. mf-bysft-interleaved is the row that fixes the ORDER: s2 appears first, then s1, then s2 again, so the answer must lead with s2 carrying both of its pairs. theta:dedup keeps the LAST occurrence, so a plain dedup would answer s1 first. That fixes what THIS def answers; it is not a claim about the emitted store, since machine_fold sorts the groups itself before writing. What the store depends on is the order WITHIN each group, which stays the order the statuses were written -- s2 carrying its pairs a then c, never c then a.",
DEF("case:mf-bysft", S2(A("system:mf_bysft"), S3(S3(A("s1"), A("e1"), A("open")), S3(A("s1"), A("e2"), A("done")), S3(A("s2"), A("e3"), A("x"))))),
DEF("case:mf-bysft-interleaved", S2(A("system:mf_bysft"), S3(S3(A("s2"), A("a"), A("p")), S3(A("s1"), A("b"), A("q")), S3(A("s2"), A("c"), A("r"))))),

"system:mf_evpos answers WHICH role the governed player fills. The store is built so TaskFinished has Task in TWO roles, positions 1 and 3, so mf-evpos pins FIRST match wins rather than last or ambiguous. mf-evpos-second reads Person at position 2, which is the row that fails if anyone assumes the governed role is position 1 -- the assumption that reads plausibly and produces events for the wrong entities, since position 1 of that fact type holds a different player entirely.",
DEF("case:mf-evpos", S2(A("system:mf_evpos"), S2(S2(A("TaskFinished"), A("Task")), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("role"), S4(S4(A("r1"), A("TaskFinished"), N(1), A("Task")), S4(A("r2"), A("TaskFinished"), N(2), A("Person")), S4(A("r3"), A("Other"), N(1), A("Task")), S4(A("r4"), A("TaskFinished"), N(3), A("Task")))))))),
DEF("case:mf-evpos-second", S2(A("system:mf_evpos"), S2(S2(A("TaskFinished"), A("Person")), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("role"), S4(S4(A("r1"), A("TaskFinished"), N(1), A("Task")), S4(A("r2"), A("TaskFinished"), N(2), A("Person")), S4(A("r3"), A("Other"), N(1), A("Task")), S4(A("r4"), A("TaskFinished"), N(3), A("Task")))))))),

"system:replay_barrier decides which replay entries may not be batched. The three rows are the three answers that matter: a plain install batches, the SAME op on a trigger fact type does not, and a non-install barriers whatever its fact type. The middle row is the one the bug was: replay answered an entity INITIAL status where sequential application answered the transitioned one, because a trigger entry had been batched as though installing the fact were the whole of its meaning.",
DEF("case:replay-barrier-plain", S2(A("system:replay_barrier"), S2(S2(A("apply"), A("Person_has_Name")), S2(A("TaskFinished"), A("OrderShipped"))))),
DEF("case:replay-barrier-trigger", S2(A("system:replay_barrier"), S2(S2(A("apply"), A("TaskFinished")), S2(A("TaskFinished"), A("OrderShipped"))))),
DEF("case:replay-barrier-retract", S2(A("system:replay_barrier"), S2(S2(A("retract"), A("Person_has_Name")), S2(A("TaskFinished"), A("OrderShipped"))))),

"system:classify_order evaluated whole. The case matters more than it looks: the order decides which pattern claims a statement when two could match, so pinning it per host makes the 43-slot sequence byte-identical across the fleet rather than merely present. The python side is compared against compiler.py _CLASSIFY by the coverage gate, which was shown to FAIL on a two-element swap before being trusted.",
DEF("case:classify-order", S2(A("system:classify_order"), PHI())),

"system:id_sentinel refuses a write whose KEY is the phi atom or the empty string. Five rows, and three of them are the ones a looser guard gets wrong. id-sentinel-late carries the empty string in the SECOND position and is allowed: only position 1 is the key, and refusing on any empty field would reject ordinary optional values. id-sentinel-norow is the empty row, which answers false rather than bottom -- a row with no first element is not a row with a bad key, and collapsing the two makes the refusals indistinguishable at the boundary. id-sentinel-phi is the phi ATOM rather than the three characters, which is what actually leaked in the 2026-07-08 phantom.",
DEF("case:id-sentinel-ok", S2(A("system:id_sentinel"), S2(A("t1"), A("x")))),
DEF("case:id-sentinel-empty", S2(A("system:id_sentinel"), S2(A(""), A("x")))),
DEF("case:id-sentinel-phi", S2(A("system:id_sentinel"), S2(A("φ"), A("x")))),
DEF("case:id-sentinel-norow", S2(A("system:id_sentinel"), PHI())),
DEF("case:id-sentinel-late", S2(A("system:id_sentinel"), S2(A("a"), A("")))),

"system:write_refused, and write-refused-unchanged is the row that carries the rule. The create answered ok and the store came back IDENTICAL, and that is a refusal: an install leaving D unchanged did not install, whatever it answered. Treating it as committed appends a step to the event log for a fact the store does not hold, and a replay then diverges from the store it claims to reconstruct. write-refused-error is the plain refusal and write-committed the only row that commits -- answered ok AND the store actually grew.",
DEF("case:write-refused-error", S2(A("system:write_refused"), S3(A("ERROR"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("P"), S1(S1(A("a"))))), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("P"), S1(S1(A("a")))))))),
DEF("case:write-refused-unchanged", S2(A("system:write_refused"), S3(A("ok"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("P"), S1(S1(A("a"))))), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("P"), S1(S1(A("a")))))))),
DEF("case:write-committed", S2(A("system:write_refused"), S3(A("ok"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("P"), S1(S1(A("a"))))), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("P"), S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b")))))))),

"system:role1_player answers a fact type SUBJECT, the player at role position 1. role1-player-norole1 is a fact type whose only role sits at position 2, and it REFUSES rather than answering that player: a fact type with no role at position 1 has no subject to birth, and answering the position-2 player would seed a status against a key nobody wrote. The store also holds a second role of the SAME fact type at position 2, so the first case shows the position selecting rather than the fact type merely matching.",
DEF("case:role1-player", S2(A("system:role1_player"), S2(A("TaskHasSubject"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("role"), S3(S4(A("r1"), A("TaskHasSubject"), N(1), A("Task")), S4(A("r2"), A("TaskHasSubject"), N(2), A("Text")), S4(A("r3"), A("Other"), N(2), A("Person")))))))),
DEF("case:role1-player-norole1", S2(A("system:role1_player"), S2(A("Other"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("role"), S3(S4(A("r1"), A("TaskHasSubject"), N(1), A("Task")), S4(A("r2"), A("TaskHasSubject"), N(2), A("Text")), S4(A("r3"), A("Other"), N(2), A("Person")))))))),

"system:governing_noun, and governing-noun-self is the row that carries the rule: a noun the closure never mentions answers ITSELF. That is the ordinary case rather than a lookup failure -- a noun without subtyping governs itself -- so the same lookup serves governed and ungoverned nouns and the caller needs no branch. A def answering phi there would make every ungoverned noun look like a miss, and the write path would stop birthing statuses for exactly the entities that have no subtyping at all.",
DEF("case:governing-noun", S2(A("system:governing_noun"), S2(A("Manager"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("governedBy"), S2(S2(A("Manager"), A("Employee")), S2(A("Contractor"), A("Person")))))))),
DEF("case:governing-noun-self", S2(A("system:governing_noun"), S2(A("Task"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("governedBy"), S2(S2(A("Manager"), A("Employee")), S2(A("Contractor"), A("Person")))))))),

"system:status_ft answers which column holds a noun machine status. status-ft-none is a noun with no machine and answers PHI, deliberately unlike system:role1_player, which BOTTOMS on its miss. The two misses are different kinds of thing: most nouns in most apps have no machine, while a fact type with no role at position 1 is a compile fault. Answering them alike would either bottom every ordinary write or swallow a real fault.",
DEF("case:status-ft", S2(A("system:status_ft"), S2(A("Task"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("smStatusFt"), S2(S2(A("Task"), A("Task_has_Status")), S2(A("Order"), A("Order_has_Status")))))))),
DEF("case:status-ft-none", S2(A("system:status_ft"), S2(A("Person"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("smStatusFt"), S2(S2(A("Task"), A("Task_has_Status")), S2(A("Order"), A("Order_has_Status")))))))),

"system:machine_of takes the GOVERNING noun machine first and falls back to the noun. machine-of is a subtype governed by a supertype: Manager has no machine of its own and must take the Employee one, which is why the governing noun is tried FIRST -- reversing the order seeds the wrong initial status wherever a subtype happens to carry a machine name too. machine-of-fallback has a governing noun with no machine and a noun that has one, so the fallback is not decoration: system:governing_noun answers a noun ITSELF when no closure mentions it, and the machine may sit under that name. machine-of-none answers phi.",
DEF("case:machine-of", S2(A("system:machine_of"), S2(S2(A("Employee"), A("Manager")), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("smDef"), S2(S2(A("EmpMachine"), A("Employee")), S2(A("TaskMachine"), A("Task")))))))),
DEF("case:machine-of-fallback", S2(A("system:machine_of"), S2(S2(A("Person"), A("Task")), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("smDef"), S2(S2(A("EmpMachine"), A("Employee")), S2(A("TaskMachine"), A("Task")))))))),
DEF("case:machine-of-none", S2(A("system:machine_of"), S2(S2(A("Person"), A("Nobody")), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("smDef"), S2(S2(A("EmpMachine"), A("Employee")), S2(A("TaskMachine"), A("Task")))))))),

"system:initial_status reads the DECLARED initial population, not the effective one. A machine may also have a ROOTED status the base derives from having no inbound transition, and that is a fact about the graph rather than a statement about where an entity starts, so a birth lands the declared initial. initial-status-none is a machine nobody declared a start for: it answers phi and the write path births nothing, because picking the first status by position would make the seeded status depend on authoring order.",
DEF("case:initial-status", S2(A("system:initial_status"), S2(A("TaskMachine"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("StatusIsInitialInStateMachineDefinition"), S2(S2(A("open"), A("TaskMachine")), S2(A("draft"), A("OrderMachine")))))))),
DEF("case:initial-status-none", S2(A("system:initial_status"), S2(A("NoMachine"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("StatusIsInitialInStateMachineDefinition"), S2(S2(A("open"), A("TaskMachine")), S2(A("draft"), A("OrderMachine")))))))),

"system:has_status is the guard that makes a birth happen ONCE, and has-status-moved is the row that fixes WHAT it matches. t2 has already moved on to done, and it still answers true, because the match is on the KEY at position 1 and not on the row. Comparing rows would find no match for an entity that has advanced, and the write path would then birth a second, contradictory status row for it -- an entity both done and freshly open. has-status-absent is the key no row holds, the only shape that births.",
DEF("case:has-status-moved", S2(A("system:has_status"), S2(S2(A("Task_has_Status"), A("t2")), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("Task_has_Status"), S2(S2(A("t1"), A("open")), S2(A("t2"), A("done")))))))),
DEF("case:has-status-absent", S2(A("system:has_status"), S2(S2(A("Task_has_Status"), A("t9")), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("Task_has_Status"), S2(S2(A("t1"), A("open")), S2(A("t2"), A("done")))))))),

"system:slot_filled reads an absorbed wide row slot. slot-hash is the row that separates this from every other absent test in the system: the HASH is the wide row own marker for a column the layout carries but this entity does not fill, and it appears in NO sparse population. A check written against the sparse markers -- empty and phi -- reads that hash as a value and refuses to seed a status the entity does not have. slot-short is the ragged row, too short to reach the position because the layout grew after it was written, and it answers false rather than bottom: a missing tail is an unfilled slot, not a fault.",
DEF("case:slot-filled", S2(A("system:slot_filled"), S2(S2(A("t1"), A("open")), N(2)))),
DEF("case:slot-hash", S2(A("system:slot_filled"), S2(S2(A("t1"), A("#")), N(2)))),
DEF("case:slot-short", S2(A("system:slot_filled"), S2(S1(A("t1")), N(2)))),

"system:noun_roles lists the roles a noun FILLS. The store holds a RING fact type -- Person at position 1 and again at position 2 of P_manages_P -- so the case shows both rows surviving with the same reading and different positions. Drop the position and a ring says the same thing twice with no way to tell manager from managed. P_has_Name has no reading and still appears, carrying the EMPTY template: the noun plays that role whether or not anyone wrote a reading for it, and skipping it would make participation depend on authoring. O_has_X belongs to another noun and is absent.",
DEF("case:noun-roles", S2(A("system:noun_roles"), S2(A("Person"), S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("role"), S4(S4(A("r1"), A("P_manages_P"), N(1), A("Person")), S4(A("r2"), A("P_manages_P"), N(2), A("Person")), S4(A("r3"), A("P_has_Name"), N(1), A("Person")), S4(A("r4"), A("O_has_X"), N(1), A("Order")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("factType"), S1(S2(A("P_manages_P"), A("{0} manages {1}")))))))),
DEF("case:noun-roles-none", S2(A("system:noun_roles"), S2(A("Nobody"), S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("role"), S4(S4(A("r1"), A("P_manages_P"), N(1), A("Person")), S4(A("r2"), A("P_manages_P"), N(2), A("Person")), S4(A("r3"), A("P_has_Name"), N(1), A("Person")), S4(A("r4"), A("O_has_X"), N(1), A("Order")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("factType"), S1(S2(A("P_manages_P"), A("{0} manages {1}")))))))),

"system:ft_table answers which table holds a fact type rows. The partition pairs read <FACT TYPE, table>, which is the order rmap_partition hands over -- its dict is keyed by fact type -- and system:table_columns settles it independently: that def filters on column 2 and projects column 1. Both this def and system:col_pos were first written with the columns REVERSED and passed, because the case literal was hand-built in the assumed order rather than taken from the host. ft-table-own is the rule rather than an edge: a fact type absent from the partition has its OWN table, because rule 1 gives a fact type with a spanning uniqueness constraint or none at all a table of its own, so being unabsorbed IS the routing. Answering phi there would make every rule-1 fact type look unrouted and the write path would have nowhere to put the commonest shape in any model.",
DEF("case:ft-table", S2(A("system:ft_table"), S2(A("Person_has_Name"), S3(S2(A("Person_has_Name"), A("Person")), S2(A("Person_has_Age"), A("Person")), S2(A("Order_has_Date"), A("Order")))))),
DEF("case:ft-table-own", S2(A("system:ft_table"), S2(A("Standalone_ft"), S3(S2(A("Person_has_Name"), A("Person")), S2(A("Person_has_Age"), A("Person")), S2(A("Order_has_Date"), A("Order")))))),

"system:is_trigger reads the SECOND column of smTrigger, and is-trigger-machine is the row that proves it. TaskMachine is a MACHINE name, sitting in column one of every row, and it answers false: a test written against the first column would call it a trigger and would call every fact type a trigger in an app with a single machine. The question decides real branches -- a trigger takes the gated create rather than the bulk install, and create_spec builds it machine, mealy and links recipes a plain fact type never gets.",
DEF("case:is-trigger", S2(A("system:is_trigger"), S2(A("TaskFinished"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("smTrigger"), S2(S2(A("TaskMachine"), A("TaskFinished")), S2(A("OrderMachine"), A("OrderShipped")))))))),
DEF("case:is-trigger-machine", S2(A("system:is_trigger"), S2(A("TaskMachine"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("smTrigger"), S2(S2(A("TaskMachine"), A("TaskFinished")), S2(A("OrderMachine"), A("OrderShipped")))))))),
DEF("case:is-trigger-plain", S2(A("system:is_trigger"), S2(A("Person_has_Name"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("smTrigger"), S2(S2(A("TaskMachine"), A("TaskFinished")), S2(A("OrderMachine"), A("OrderShipped")))))))),

"system:trigger_fts answers the trigger fact types distinct and SORTED. The store lists TaskFinished before OrderShipped and repeats the first, so the case shows both the dedup and the ordering: rmap:distinct_at preserves dedup order rather than sorting -- given b then a it answers b then a -- so alphabetical here is asked for, not inherited. It matters because this list drives a greedy walk whose answer depends on the order events are considered in, and an unsorted set would make the fold depend on the order fact types happened to be declared.",
DEF("case:trigger-fts", S2(A("system:trigger_fts"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("smTrigger"), S3(S2(A("M1"), A("TaskFinished")), S2(A("M2"), A("OrderShipped")), S2(A("M1"), A("TaskFinished"))))))),

"system:ft_roles answers a fact type players in ROLE ORDER. The store lists role 2 BEFORE role 1 on purpose, so the case shows the def sorting rather than inheriting store order. Both roles are Person, which is why the sort is by POSITION and not by player: sorted by name the manager and the managed would come back in whichever order the rows happened to be stored, and the reading template fills its slots by position. A fact type with no roles answers the empty sequence.",
DEF("case:ft-roles", S2(A("system:ft_roles"), S2(A("P_manages_P"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("role"), S3(S4(A("r2"), A("P_manages_P"), N(2), A("Person")), S4(A("r1"), A("P_manages_P"), N(1), A("Person")), S4(A("r3"), A("G"), N(1), A("Order")))))))),
DEF("case:ft-roles-absent", S2(A("system:ft_roles"), S2(A("Nope"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("role"), S3(S4(A("r2"), A("P_manages_P"), N(2), A("Person")), S4(A("r1"), A("P_manages_P"), N(1), A("Person")), S4(A("r3"), A("G"), N(1), A("Order")))))))),

"system:ft_arity answers a fact type highest role position. ft-arity-ternary holds its roles at 3 and 1, out of order, so the answer 3 shows the ordering being used rather than the store order. ft-arity-none is the fact type no role row mentions and answers TWO -- not zero, not phi -- because the caller asks this to decide UNARY, and a fact type whose roles have not been read must not compile as unary and take a different row shape entirely. ft-arity-unary is the one that genuinely is.",
DEF("case:ft-arity-unary", S2(A("system:ft_arity"), S2(A("U"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("role"), S5(S4(A("r2"), A("F"), N(2), A("Q")), S4(A("r1"), A("F"), N(1), A("P")), S4(A("r3"), A("U"), N(1), A("P")), S4(A("r4"), A("T"), N(3), A("Z")), S4(A("r5"), A("T"), N(1), A("A")))))))),
DEF("case:ft-arity-ternary", S2(A("system:ft_arity"), S2(A("T"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("role"), S5(S4(A("r2"), A("F"), N(2), A("Q")), S4(A("r1"), A("F"), N(1), A("P")), S4(A("r3"), A("U"), N(1), A("P")), S4(A("r4"), A("T"), N(3), A("Z")), S4(A("r5"), A("T"), N(1), A("A")))))))),
DEF("case:ft-arity-none", S2(A("system:ft_arity"), S2(A("Nope"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("role"), S5(S4(A("r2"), A("F"), N(2), A("Q")), S4(A("r1"), A("F"), N(1), A("P")), S4(A("r3"), A("U"), N(1), A("P")), S4(A("r4"), A("T"), N(3), A("Z")), S4(A("r5"), A("T"), N(1), A("A")))))))),

"system:col_pos answers where an absorbed fact type sits in its wide row. col-pos-first answers TWO, not one, and that is the whole content: the 3NF row is <key, v1, v2, ...>, so the key holds position 1 and the first absorbed column position 2. col-pos-second answers three, so the two together fix both the offset and the declaration order. A fact type absorbed into no column bottoms rather than answering a position -- writing its value at a guessed column would overwrite whichever fact type actually lives there.",
DEF("case:col-pos-first", S2(A("system:col_pos"), S2(S2(A("Person"), A("Person_has_Name")), S3(S2(A("Person_has_Name"), A("Person")), S2(A("Person_has_Age"), A("Person")), S2(A("Order_has_Date"), A("Order")))))),
DEF("case:col-pos-second", S2(A("system:col_pos"), S2(S2(A("Person"), A("Person_has_Age")), S3(S2(A("Person_has_Name"), A("Person")), S2(A("Person_has_Age"), A("Person")), S2(A("Order_has_Date"), A("Order")))))),

"The subtype lattice, both directions, asked of the SAME noun. Employee sits in the middle of the store -- a subtype of Person and a supertype of Manager and Intern -- so supertypes-of and subtypes-of answer differently for it and neither is the other reversed. A subtype row is <subtype, supertype>: the supertypes of X are the second column of rows whose FIRST is X, and the subtypes of X the first column of rows whose SECOND is X. They are separate defs because one def with a direction flag lets a caller pass the wrong flag and get a plausible answer, both being noun names. supertypes-of-root is the top of the lattice, answering empty rather than bottom.",
DEF("case:supertypes-of", S2(A("system:supertypes_of"), S2(A("Employee"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("subtype"), S4(S2(A("Manager"), A("Employee")), S2(A("Contractor"), A("Person")), S2(A("Employee"), A("Person")), S2(A("Intern"), A("Employee")))))))),
DEF("case:subtypes-of", S2(A("system:subtypes_of"), S2(A("Employee"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("subtype"), S4(S2(A("Manager"), A("Employee")), S2(A("Contractor"), A("Person")), S2(A("Employee"), A("Person")), S2(A("Intern"), A("Employee")))))))),
DEF("case:supertypes-of-root", S2(A("system:supertypes_of"), S2(A("Person"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("subtype"), S4(S2(A("Manager"), A("Employee")), S2(A("Contractor"), A("Person")), S2(A("Employee"), A("Person")), S2(A("Intern"), A("Employee")))))))),

"system:vals_of answers EVERY value a key holds, and vals-of-many is why it exists: Task is an instance of two things and one of them is written twice. csdp:lookup and rmap:lookup0 both answer the FIRST match only -- one a scalar, one a one-tuple -- so either would report Task as an ObjectType and silently drop that it is also a Thing. The duplicate is deduped and the answer sorted, because what a key IS is a set rather than a history of how its rows were written.",
DEF("case:vals-of-many", S2(A("system:vals_of"), S2(S2(A("instanceOf"), A("Task")), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("instanceOf"), S4(S2(A("Task"), A("Thing")), S2(A("Task"), A("ObjectType")), S2(A("Task"), A("ObjectType")), S2(A("Name"), A("ValueType")))))))),
DEF("case:vals-of-absent", S2(A("system:vals_of"), S2(S2(A("instanceOf"), A("Nope")), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("instanceOf"), S4(S2(A("Task"), A("Thing")), S2(A("Task"), A("ObjectType")), S2(A("Task"), A("ObjectType")), S2(A("Name"), A("ValueType")))))))),

"system:governance_plans evaluated whole: the two rules that MAKE the governedBy closure, as data -- name, what it reads, how it projects, and what it derives. The base rule reads smDef and projects <2,1>, turning <machine, noun> into <noun, machine>; the step rule reads subtype beside the closure being built and projects <1,3>, which is what makes it recursive. Cased whole rather than per field because the ORDER is part of the answer -- base before step, so the first round has something to close over -- and a case that checked membership would pass with the two reversed. The DERIVED fact type is in the row because the host otherwise states it three times: in the plan, in the ruleReads it appends, and in the ruleDerives. Both of those tuples are built from these rows now, so a third rule is one row here and nothing else anywhere.",
DEF("case:governance-plans", S2(A("system:governance_plans"), PHI())),
DEF("case:reading-parse-hyphen", S2(A("system:reading_parse"), S3(A("Layer has valence- Coord -local and FORE-- WORD and from-Status"), S2(A("Layer"), A("Coord")), S2(A("The"), A("Each"))))),
DEF("case:reading-ftid", S2(A("system:ftid"), S2(A("{0} was placed by {1}"), S2(A("Order"), A("Customer"))))),

DEF("case:skolem-mints-frontier-id", S2(A("skolem"), S2(A("menu"), A("t1")))),

DEF("case:skolem-empty-frontier-bottoms", S2(A("skolem"), PHI())),

DEF("case:escape-html-entities", S2(A("escape_html"), A("a<b>&\"c"))),

DEF("case:escape-html-int-refuses", S2(A("escape_html"), N(7))),

DEF("case:sqlcol-unary-strips-noun",
    S2(A("system:sqlcol"), S6(A("unary"), A("Task"), A("Task_is_started"),
                              A("#"), A("#"), N(1)))),

DEF("case:sqlcol-ref-joins-mode",
    S2(A("system:sqlcol"), S6(A("ref"), A("Order"),
                              A("Order_was_placed_by_Customer"),
                              A("Customer"), A("nr"), N(1)))),

DEF("case:sqlcol-value-names-player",
    S2(A("system:sqlcol"), S6(A("value"), A("Task"), A("Task_has_Task_Priority"),
                              A("Task Priority"), A("#"), N(1)))),

DEF("case:sqlcol-dedup-suffixes-second",
    S2(A("system:sqlcol"), S6(A("value"), A("Task"), A("Task_has_Task_Priority"),
                              A("Task Priority"), A("#"), N(2)))),

DEF("case:entity-view-canonical",
    S2(A("system:entity_view"),
       S3(A("Task"), A("t1"),
          S6(S3(A("CELL"), A("rmapColumns"),
                S2(S3(A("Task"), N(2), A("Task_is_started")),
                   S3(A("Task"), N(3), A("Task_has_Task_Priority")))),
             S3(A("CELL"), A("role"),
                S3(S4(A("r1"), A("Task_is_started"), N(1), A("Task")),
                   S4(A("r2"), A("Task_has_Task_Priority"), N(1), A("Task")),
                   S4(A("r3"), A("Task_has_Task_Priority"), N(2),
                      A("Task Priority")))),
             S3(A("CELL"), A("factType"),
                S2(S2(A("Task_is_started"), A("{0} is started")),
                   S2(A("Task_has_Task_Priority"), A("{0} has {1}")))),
             S3(A("CELL"), A("Task"), S2(S1(A("t1")), S1(A("t2")))),
             S3(A("CELL"), A("Task_is_started"), S1(S1(A("t1")))),
             S3(A("CELL"), A("Task_has_Task_Priority"),
                S2(S2(A("t1"), A("p0")), S2(A("t1"), A("p2")))))))),

"THE ABSORBING TABLE DOES NOT ALWAYS PLAY ROLE 1. A single-role uniqueness constraint absorbs a fact type into the table of the player at rmap:keypos -- the position the UC sits on -- and that is role 2 whenever the UC is. Here Message plays role 2 of Support_Response_responds_to_Message, so m1 must be matched at position 2 and the value taken from position 1: the answer is s1. ev_fpred2 compared position 1 and ev_fval took position 2 unconditionally, which answers # and unseen for this store -- it type checks, it reduces, and it silently reports that the message has no response. support.auto.dev is the corpus instance; the other 1885 absorptions do play role 1, which is why role 1 passed for so long.",
DEF("case:entity-view-role2-key",
    S2(A("system:entity_view"),
       S3(A("Message"), A("m1"),
          S5(S3(A("CELL"), A("rmapColumns"),
                S1(S3(A("Message"), N(2),
                      A("Support_Response_responds_to_Message")))),
             S3(A("CELL"), A("role"),
                S2(S4(A("r1"),
                      A("Support_Response_responds_to_Message"), N(1),
                      A("Support Response")),
                   S4(A("r2"),
                      A("Support_Response_responds_to_Message"), N(2),
                      A("Message")))),
             S3(A("CELL"), A("factType"),
                S1(S2(A("Support_Response_responds_to_Message"),
                      A("{0} responds to {1}")))),
             S3(A("CELL"), A("Message"), S2(S1(A("m1")), S1(A("m2")))),
             S3(A("CELL"), A("Support_Response_responds_to_Message"),
                S2(S2(A("s1"), A("m1")), S2(A("s2"), A("m2")))))))),

"rmap:cone is the SUBTYPE CONE of a table: every type whose chain of supertypes reaches it, plus itself. cancel-service declares Resource -> Noun -> Function and Status -> Resource, so asking for Function must reach Status TRANSITIVELY -- a one-hop reading answers Noun and Verb and stops, which is what made the projection populate function with seven ids while 521 more belonged in it. The unrelated pair (Wheel, Car) must not appear, and Car must not either: a cone is not every supertype in the store. The second case pins the leaf, whose cone is only itself, so a version answering the whole subtype graph cannot pass.",
DEF("case:rmap-cone-transitive",
    S2(A("rmap:cone"),
       S2(A("Function"),
          S5(S2(A("Noun"), A("Function")),
             S2(A("Verb"), A("Function")),
             S2(A("Resource"), A("Noun")),
             S2(A("Status"), A("Resource")),
             S2(A("Wheel"), A("Car")))))),

DEF("case:rmap-cone-leaf",
    S2(A("rmap:cone"),
       S2(A("Status"),
          S5(S2(A("Noun"), A("Function")),
             S2(A("Verb"), A("Function")),
             S2(A("Resource"), A("Noun")),
             S2(A("Status"), A("Resource")),
             S2(A("Wheel"), A("Car")))))),

DEF("case:rmap-conepos",
    S2(A("rmap:conepos"),
       S2(S2(A("Function"), A("Status")),
          S2(S2(A("Status_has_HTTP_Method"),
                S2(S2(N(1), A("Status")), S2(N(2), A("HTTP Method")))),
             S2(A("Car_has_Wheel"),
                S2(S2(N(1), A("Car")), S2(N(2), A("Wheel")))))))),

"A SUBTYPE NOUN HAS NO TABLE OF ITS OWN. Status is a subtype of Function, so Status_has_HTTP_Method absorbs into the FUNCTION table and the rmapColumns row names Function, not Status. Asked for Status, a reading that filters those rows by the noun's own name finds none and answers no fields and unseen -- which is what python get_view did to cancel-service, 0 fields for Status where Function answered 18. The answer must be the column the supertype table carries, because a Status IS a Function under the same identifier. engine/rust had this resolution natively (ev_cols_native, 2026-07-08) and canon did not, so it is the one capability fat rust held that the other four hosts could not reach.",
DEF("case:entity-view-subtype-noun",
    S2(A("system:entity_view"),
       S3(A("Status"), A("deleted"),
          S6(S3(A("CELL"), A("subtype"),
                S1(S2(A("Status"), A("Function")))),
             S3(A("CELL"), A("rmapColumns"),
                S1(S3(A("Function"), N(2),
                      A("Status_has_HTTP_Method")))),
             S3(A("CELL"), A("role"),
                S2(S4(A("r1"), A("Status_has_HTTP_Method"), N(1),
                      A("Status")),
                   S4(A("r2"), A("Status_has_HTTP_Method"), N(2),
                      A("HTTP Method")))),
             S3(A("CELL"), A("factType"),
                S1(S2(A("Status_has_HTTP_Method"), A("{0} has {1}")))),
             S3(A("CELL"), A("Status"), S1(S1(A("deleted")))),
             S3(A("CELL"), A("Status_has_HTTP_Method"),
                S1(S2(A("deleted"), A("DELETE")))))))),

"COMPILATION EVALUATED AS CANON, and what it answers rather than that it answered. law:reader_match already asserts read:parse over the six FORML readings in read:witness_rows EQUALS read:witness_expected, so every host has been compiling FORML to fact types all along -- but a law answers T, and T does not say what was built. This case carries the output itself: the fact type SaleContainsItemInQuantity over <Sale, Item, Quantity> with the uniqueness <<1,2>> the sixth reading constrains. The ALPHA is the law's own preprocessing and is LOAD-BEARING: a row is <kind, <clause, clause...>> because the constraint reading splits across two clauses, and read:parse wants the clauses flattened into one token run. Handed the rows unflattened it answers bottom, which reads exactly like a broken parser and is a caller that did not read its callee.",
DEF("case:read-parse-witness",
    S2(S4(A("COMP"), A("read:parse"),
          S2(A("ALPHA"),
             S3(A("CONS"), N(1),
                S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:flatten"), N(2)))),
          A("read:witness_rows")),
       PHI())),

"THE UNIFORMLY-VERBALIZED UNIQUENESS, which is the one NORMA emits. Its UniversalQuantifier snippet is `each {0}` and Halpin verbalizes a simple internal uniqueness as \"Each Person has at most one birthCountry\" throughout Information Modeling and Relational Databases; the \"For each X, that X ... at most one Y\" form read:witness_rows carries is the SPANNING one. read:row admitted only the For-each opener, so the compact form never reached read:attach_uc and its constraint was dropped WITHOUT A WORD -- 42 of spd-1's 86 constraints are uniqueness. -compact answers uc <<1>>. -separated pins ORDER INDEPENDENCE, which cost a rewrite: attach_uc used to compare against 1r, the newest fact type only, so a constraint that did not directly follow its own reading was dropped. A readings file groups its constraints after its readings -- NORMA lays a model out that way and so do spd-1's files -- so exactly one of them attached. Both arms search now: distr the fact types against the constraint and rewrite the one whose token run matches. Here BOTH SaleHasQuantity and SaleHasPrice come back with uc <<1>> from the grouped order, and the same is true of the For-each form.",
DEF("case:read-uc-compact",
    S2(S4(A("COMP"), A("read:parse"),
          S2(A("ALPHA"),
             S3(A("CONS"), N(1), S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:flatten"), N(2)))),
          A("read:witness_compact")),
       PHI())),

DEF("case:read-uc-separated",
    S2(S4(A("COMP"), A("read:parse"),
          S2(A("ALPHA"),
             S3(A("CONS"), N(1), S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:flatten"), N(2)))),
          A("read:witness_separated")),
       PHI())),

"MANDATORY, the second constraint kind by count -- 20 of spd-1's 86, after uniqueness at 42. NORMA pairs its `each {0}` universal with `some {0}`, and Halpin verbalizes it \"Each Person has some birthCountry\": the same opener as the compact uniqueness, a different quantifier. read:row already routed every kind-1 Each-row to read:attach_uc, so these arrived, matched no uniqueness pattern, and were dropped in the same silence. read:reading lays an entry out as <name, players, ucs, phi, <phi>, tokens> and set_ucs preserves fields 4 and 5, so mandatory takes field 4 with the same shape the uniqueness list has. The witness groups BOTH constraints after BOTH readings, which is the arrangement 04c99964 made survivable: Quantity comes back mandatory and unconstrained, Price unique and optional, each attached to its own reading rather than to whichever was read last.",
DEF("case:read-mandatory",
    S2(S4(A("COMP"), A("read:parse"),
          S2(A("ALPHA"),
             S3(A("CONS"), N(1), S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:flatten"), N(2)))),
          A("read:witness_mandatory")),
       PHI())),

"DECLARATION-LEVEL READINGS ARE NOT FACT TYPES. \"OSI is a subtype of Layered System\" used to fall through to the kind-0 reading arm and come back as OSIIsASubtypeOfLayeredSystem over <Layered System> -- not a constraint missed but a FACT TYPE INVENTED, a relation nobody declared, which a reader taking populations would go looking for. \"The possible values of X are ...\" -- NORMA's MultiValueValueConstraint snippet, `the possible values of {0} are {1}` -- was dropped. Both are recognized now and pass through, so this witness answers exactly ONE fact type: LayerHasPDUName with its uniqueness. RECORDING them is the next increment and needs somewhere to put them: read:parse carries <decls, fts, rows>, the decls accumulator exists only to feed read:tile's name matching, and the post projects N(2) twice and throws N(1) away. Declaration-level facts need an accumulator of their own. Not asserting a false fact type did not have to wait for that.",
DEF("case:read-declarations",
    S2(S4(A("COMP"), A("read:parse"),
          S2(A("ALPHA"),
             S3(A("CONS"), N(1), S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:flatten"), N(2)))),
          A("read:witness_declarations")),
       PHI())),

"DEONTIC CONSTRAINTS ARE CONSTRAINTS, NOT FACT TYPES, AND NOT ALETHIC ONES. Halpin verbalizes them as modal wrappers over the forms already parsed -- \"It is obligatory that each Immigrant has some Passport\", \"It is forbidden that the same Person has more than one SocialSecurityNumber\" -- so the tempting move is to strip the prefix and delegate to the mandatory and uniqueness arms. That would record a deontic constraint AS ALETHIC, and a deontic constraint is violable: an authorized override is a recorded Violation, not a modeling error. Asserting it alethically states something the model does not. The store already says where it belongs -- a constraint is its own row, <name, kind, fact type, modality> -- so each becomes its own tagged record, the mechanism declaration-level facts got at 22145510. Both come back in the name-and-players leg and NEITHER in the fact-type leg, while the alethic Each-rule beside them still attaches its uc <<1>>. Before this they reached no arm at all and were dropped.",
DEF("case:read-deontic",
    S2(S4(A("COMP"), A("read:parse"),
          S2(A("ALPHA"),
             S3(A("CONS"), N(1), S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:flatten"), N(2)))),
          A("read:witness_deontic")),
       PHI())),

"RING CONSTRAINTS, whose semantics canon has carried all along -- ring_irreflexive, ring_symmetric, ring_asymmetric, ring_antisymmetric, ring_intransitive, ring_acyclic -- with nothing able to reach them, because no arm recognized how a reading declares one. The corpus writes \"Element transforms to Element is irreflexive.\" and NORMA verbalizes the same constraint \"no Person is a parent of itself\". The KIND IS THE LAST WORD, so one arm covers the whole family instead of six guards, and the trailing `is <kind>` is trimmed before the record is built so it names the FACT TYPE and not the sentence: ElementTransformsToElement over <Element, Element>, tagged ring_irreflexive -- the store's own shape, <name, kind, fact type, modality>. Three ring readings over two fact types answer TWO fact types and three tagged records, which is the count that matters: a constraint about a fact type must not become another fact type.",
DEF("case:read-ring",
    S2(S4(A("COMP"), A("read:parse"),
          S2(A("ALPHA"),
             S3(A("CONS"), N(1), S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:flatten"), N(2)))),
          A("read:witness_ring")),
       PHI())),

"SUBSET, the last constraint kind, and the one an agency model is made of. These readings are spd-1's, not invented for the case: \"It is forbidden that Agent causes Harm\" and \"It is obligatory that Agent obeys Order if Human issues that Order\". A subset says one population must contain another -- the consequent holds wherever the antecedent does -- and its surface shape, <consequent> if <antecedent>, IS THE SHAPE OF A DERIVATION RULE. The leading marker is the only thing between them, so mis-marking a rule does not merely lose a storage mode, it turns a constraint into a rule. spd-1's subset is deontic and the store carries both facets -- subset_Human_issues_that_Order, kind subset, modality deontic -- so this tag is a PAIR where the other records carry a one-tuple, and the third leg prints field 5 whole with no change to the projection. The forbidden reading beside it stays a plain deontic_forbidden: an obligation WITH an antecedent is a subset, an obligation without one is not.",
DEF("case:read-subset",
    S2(S4(A("COMP"), A("read:parse"),
          S2(A("ALPHA"),
             S3(A("CONS"), N(1), S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:flatten"), N(2)))),
          A("read:witness_subset")),
       PHI())),

"THE DEONTIC LEDGER. An alethic constraint is verbalized in terms of impossibility -- it cannot be violated, so a state that breaks one is REFUSED. A deontic constraint is an obligation: it CAN be violated, and the violation has to be RECORDED rather than prevented. cmd:answer refuses on any violation at all, which keeps the ledger empty by never letting anything onto it, and that is the one thing a deontic constraint must not do. This is the ledger's contents, computed from the model rather than kept by hand: for every constraint whose modality is deontic and whose kind is deontic_forbidden, the population of the fact type it forbids IS the list of violations. spd-1 declares <It is forbidden that Agent causes Harm, deontic_forbidden, Agent_causes_Harm, deontic>, so every row of Agent_causes_Harm is one, and the answer is <constraint, fact type, row>. The -clean case pins that a store whose constraints are all alethic answers phi: this is inert until a model declares an obligation, which is why no other case in this table moves.",
DEF("case:deontic-ledger",
    S2(A("cmd:deontic_viols"),
       S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("constraint"),
             S2(S4(A("It is forbidden that Agent causes Harm"),
                   A("deontic_forbidden"), A("Agent_causes_Harm"),
                   A("deontic")),
                S4(A("Layer_uc"), A("uniqueness"), A("Layer_has_Name"),
                   A("alethic")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("Agent_causes_Harm"),
             S2(S2(A("a1"), A("h1")), S2(A("a2"), A("h7"))))))),

DEF("case:deontic-ledger-clean",
    S2(A("cmd:deontic_viols"),
       S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("constraint"),
             S1(S4(A("Layer_uc"), A("uniqueness"), A("Layer_has_Name"),
                   A("alethic")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("Layer_has_Name"),
             S1(S2(A("l1"), A("Physical"))))))),

"A VIOLATION IS NOT A RECORD TO KEEP -- IT IS A FACT THAT EXISTS. Samuel: the existence of the fact itself is the violation, and facts are events, so if the substrate timestamps them the time comes free. What is NOT derivable from the fact is whether anyone has dealt with it, so handling is the only thing worth asserting, and the outstanding ledger is a SUBTRACTION rather than a second bookkeeping: cmd:deontic_viols setminus the handled rows. Here Agent_causes_Harm holds <a1,h1> and <a2,h7>, both violate the forbidden constraint, <a1,h1> has been handled, and only <a2,h7> comes back. THE FACT STAYS IN ITS POPULATION either way -- it happened, and un-happening it is not on offer. This also collapses most of the alethic/deontic difference: both derive violations the same way, an alethic one cannot be handled because it cannot exist, and what runs on a deontic one is the model's business rather than the checker's.",
DEF("case:deontic-unhandled",
    S2(A("cmd:unhandled_viols"),
       S3(S3(A("CELL"), A("constraint"),
             S1(S4(A("It is forbidden that Agent causes Harm"),
                   A("deontic_forbidden"), A("Agent_causes_Harm"),
                   A("deontic")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("Agent_causes_Harm"),
             S2(S2(A("a1"), A("h1")), S2(A("a2"), A("h7")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("handled"),
             S1(S3(A("It is forbidden that Agent causes Harm"),
                   A("Agent_causes_Harm"), S2(A("a1"), A("h1")))))))),

"THE DISAMBIGUATOR COULD EMIT ONE NAME TWICE, which is its only job. The suffix came from how many times the BASE had been seen and nothing checked whether the result was free, so a,a,a_2 answered a,a_2,a_2 and a_2,a,a answered a_2,a,a_2 -- two columns of one name, which is not a table. It SEARCHES now: the first candidate not already emitted. case:coldisamb is unchanged, because with no collision the first free candidate is the one counting produced; these two are the inputs it never asked about. -after pins that an existing a_2 is stepped over (a_3, not a_2 again) and -before that a later a_2 goes to a_2_2 rather than colliding backwards. Not reachable from the corpus today -- 6 of 44 apps carry a _N name and all are constraint or rule names, not column-producing types -- so this was latent, found by asking a DEF with ONE verifying case a question that case did not ask.",
DEF("case:coldisamb-before",
    S2(A("rmap:coldisamb"), S3(A("a"), A("a"), A("a_2")))),

DEF("case:coldisamb-after",
    S2(A("rmap:coldisamb"), S3(A("a_2"), A("a"), A("a")))),

"A POPULATION IS NOT A FACT TYPE, IT IS A ROW OF ONE. read:parse compiled \"Layer 'OSI-1' has Layer Name 'Physical'\" into a FACT TYPE named Layer'OSI-1'HasLayerName'Physical', beside the real LayerHasLayerName it instantiates -- one invented fact type per instance, and the corpus holds 5541 population readings. Same class as the subtype defect: not a fact missed, a relation asserted that nobody declared. A reading carrying a QUOTED literal is an instance; strip the literals and what remains names the fact type, and the literals in order are the row. Two instances here answer ONE fact type and two population records, each tagged <population, <row>> -- the pair shape the subset arm already uses, so the third leg prints it unchanged.",
DEF("case:read-population",
    S2(S4(A("COMP"), A("read:parse"),
          S2(A("ALPHA"),
             S3(A("CONS"), N(1), S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:flatten"), N(2)))),
          A("read:witness_population")),
       PHI())),

"A FACT TYPE WHOSE POPULATION IS NOT THE WIDTH IT DECLARED. The corpus sweep found two apps projecting ZERO rows for a populated fact type -- tax-service US_State_allows_Trade_In_Credit, 42 rows in the store and none in the table, and gym-contract-service Statute_grants_Right_to_Member, 7 and none -- and neither is a projection defect. Both declare more roles than their rows carry. \"US State allows Trade In Credit.\" reads as a BINARY because Credit is a declared entity type (twice over in that app) while the readings that populate it supply one value: the model says two and means one, and a 2-column table cannot take a 1-tuple, so the projection keeps nothing and says nothing. Checkable from the store alone -- 3 of 622 populated fact types across 44 apps disagree, and all three are real, the third being External_System_has_Kind which declares ONE role and carries 2-wide rows. F_ok is the control: matching width must not be reported, or the check is a list of every fact type.",
DEF("case:probe-refmode-vt",
    S2(A("system:refmode_vt"), S3(S2(A("Task"), A("id")), PHI(), A("")))),

DEF("case:probe-refmode-ft",
    S2(A("system:refmode_ft"), S3(S2(A("Task"), A("id")), PHI(), A("")))),

DEF("case:probe-refmode-uc",
    S2(A("system:refmode_uc"), S3(S2(A("Task"), A("id")), PHI(), A("")))),

DEF("case:probe-refmode-rows",
    S2(A("system:refmode_rows"), S3(S2(A("Task"), A("id")), PHI(), A("")))),

DEF("case:refmode-expand-sha",
    S2(A("system:h_entity"), S3(S2(A("Commit"), A("sha")), PHI(), A("")))),

DEF("case:probe-refmode-objs",
    S2(A("system:refmode_objs"), S3(S2(A("Commit"), A("sha")), PHI(), A("")))),

DEF("case:probe-refmode-mand-e",
    S2(A("system:refmode_mand_e"), S3(S2(A("Commit"), A("sha")), PHI(), A("")))),

DEF("case:probe-refmode-pid",
    S2(A("system:refmode_pid"), S3(S2(A("Commit"), A("sha")), PHI(), A("")))),

DEF("case:probe-refmode-r2",
    S2(A("system:refmode_r2"), S3(S2(A("Commit"), A("sha")), PHI(), A("")))),

DEF("case:extent-of",
    S2(A("system:extent_of"), S2(A("Task"), S4(S3(A("CELL"), A("role"),
                S4(S4(A("r1"), A("Task_has_Title"), N(1), A("Task")),
                   S4(A("r2"), A("Task_has_Title"), N(2), A("Title")),
                   S4(A("r3"), A("Task_has_Cost"), N(1), A("Task")),
                   S4(A("r4"), A("Task_has_Cost"), N(2), A("Cost")))),
             S3(A("CELL"), A("Task_has_Title"),
                S2(S2(A("t1"), A("A")), S2(A("t2"), A("B")))),
             S3(A("CELL"), A("Task_has_Cost"),
                S2(S2(A("t2"), A("9")), S2(A("t3"), A("7")))),
             S3(A("CELL"), A("instanceOf"), S1(S2(A("Task"), A("ObjectType")))))))),

DEF("case:extent-of-value",
    S2(A("system:extent_of"), S2(A("Title"), S4(S3(A("CELL"), A("role"),
                S4(S4(A("r1"), A("Task_has_Title"), N(1), A("Task")),
                   S4(A("r2"), A("Task_has_Title"), N(2), A("Title")),
                   S4(A("r3"), A("Task_has_Cost"), N(1), A("Task")),
                   S4(A("r4"), A("Task_has_Cost"), N(2), A("Cost")))),
             S3(A("CELL"), A("Task_has_Title"),
                S2(S2(A("t1"), A("A")), S2(A("t2"), A("B")))),
             S3(A("CELL"), A("Task_has_Cost"),
                S2(S2(A("t2"), A("9")), S2(A("t3"), A("7")))),
             S3(A("CELL"), A("instanceOf"), S1(S2(A("Task"), A("ObjectType")))))))),

DEF("case:ident-rows",
    S2(A("system:ident_rows"), S2(A("Task"), S4(S3(A("CELL"), A("role"),
                S4(S4(A("r1"), A("Task_has_Title"), N(1), A("Task")),
                   S4(A("r2"), A("Task_has_Title"), N(2), A("Title")),
                   S4(A("r3"), A("Task_has_Cost"), N(1), A("Task")),
                   S4(A("r4"), A("Task_has_Cost"), N(2), A("Cost")))),
             S3(A("CELL"), A("Task_has_Title"),
                S2(S2(A("t1"), A("A")), S2(A("t2"), A("B")))),
             S3(A("CELL"), A("Task_has_Cost"),
                S2(S2(A("t2"), A("9")), S2(A("t3"), A("7")))),
             S3(A("CELL"), A("instanceOf"), S1(S2(A("Task"), A("ObjectType")))))))),

DEF("case:refmode-expand",
    S2(A("system:h_entity"), S3(S2(A("Task"), A("id")), PHI(), A("")))),

DEF("case:refmode-none",
    S2(A("system:h_entity"), S3(S2(A("Task"), A("")), PHI(), A("")))),

"THE FIELD-TYPE CASES HOLD A STORE IN THE SHAPE EVERY STORE HAS (2026-09-07): the fact types' players in state:declared, the object types' kinds in state:otmeta, and the populations as FILE tables - Object Type has Enum Values for the enumerated value type (the reading's own quoted list), Object Type has Conceptual Data Type for the declared one, Entity Type has Reference Mode for the identified entities. The earlier cases held `role`, `instanceOf`, `refMode`, `constraint` and `Value_Type_has_Conceptual_Data_Type` cells, the old engine's tables, and were the only stores in that shape the lookups ever read.",

DEF("fixture:field-store",
    K(S3(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:declared"),
            S1(S4(S2(A("Task_is_closed_by_Commit"), S2(A("Task"), A("Commit"))),
                  S2(A("Task_has_Due_Date"), S2(A("Task"), A("Due Date"))),
                  S2(A("Task_has_Task_State"), S2(A("Task"), A("Task State"))),
                  S2(A("Task_has_Title"), S2(A("Task"), A("Title")))))),
         S3(A("CELL"), A("state:otmeta"),
            S1(S5(S3(A("Commit"), A("entity"), A("F")),
                  S3(A("Due Date"), A("value"), A("F")),
                  S3(A("Task"), A("entity"), A("F")),
                  S3(A("Task State"), A("value"), A("F")),
                  S3(A("Title"), A("value"), A("F"))))),
         S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"),
            S3(S3(A("CELL"), A("ObjectTypeHasEnumValues"),
                  S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("Task State"), S1(A("'open', 'done', 'blocked'"))))),
               S3(A("CELL"), A("ObjectTypeHasConceptualDataType"),
                  S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("Due Date"), S1(A("date"))))),
               S3(A("CELL"), A("EntityTypeHasReferenceMode"),
                  S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("Task"), S1(A("id"))),
                     S3(A("CELL"), A("Commit"), S1(A("sha")))))))))),

DEF("case:field-type-entity",
    S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("ui:field_type"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("Task_is_closed_by_Commit")), A("fixture:field-store"))), PHI())),

DEF("case:field-refmode",
    S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("ui:ref_mode_of"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("Task_is_closed_by_Commit")), A("fixture:field-store"))), PHI())),

DEF("case:field-type-declared",
    S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("ui:field_type"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("Task_has_Due_Date")), A("fixture:field-store"))), PHI())),

DEF("case:field-type-enum",
    S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("ui:field_type"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("Task_has_Task_State")), A("fixture:field-store"))), PHI())),

DEF("case:field-type-text",
    S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("ui:field_type"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("Task_has_Title")), A("fixture:field-store"))), PHI())),

DEF("case:field-opts-enum",
    S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("ui:field_opts"), S4(A("CONS"), K(A("Task_has_Task_State")), A("fixture:field-store"), K(A("enum")))), PHI())),

DEF("case:field-opts-text",
    S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("ui:field_opts"), S4(A("CONS"), K(A("Task_has_Title")), A("fixture:field-store"), K(A("text")))), PHI())),

DEF("case:enum-values-split",
    S2(A("ui:enum_values"), A("'Data Link', 'x', 'a, b'"))),

DEF("case:control-edit-date",
    S2(A("ui:control_for"), S2(A("edit"), A("date")))),

DEF("case:control-edit-enum",
    S2(A("ui:control_for"), S2(A("edit"), A("enum")))),

DEF("case:control-edit-bool",
    S2(A("ui:control_for"), S2(A("edit"), A("boolean")))),

DEF("case:control-edit-entity",
    S2(A("ui:control_for"), S2(A("edit"), A("entity")))),

DEF("case:control-edit-money",
    S2(A("ui:control_for"), S2(A("edit"), A("money")))),

DEF("case:control-view-entity",
    S2(A("ui:control_for"), S2(A("view"), A("entity")))),

DEF("case:control-edit-unknown",
    S2(A("ui:control_for"), S2(A("edit"), A("GeoPoint")))),

DEF("case:control-view-date",
    S2(A("ui:control_for"), S2(A("view"), A("date")))),

DEF("case:control-list-title",
    S2(A("ui:control_for"), S2(A("list"), A("title")))),

DEF("case:control-list-other",
    S2(A("ui:control_for"), S2(A("list"), A("date")))),

DEF("case:control-edit-largetext",
    S2(A("ui:control_for"), S2(A("edit"), A("largeText")))),

DEF("case:check-arity",
    S2(A("check:arity"),
       S4(S3(A("CELL"), A("role"),
             S5(S4(A("r1"), A("F_ok"), N(1), A("A")),
                S4(A("r2"), A("F_ok"), N(2), A("B")),
                S4(A("r3"), A("F_bad"), N(1), A("A")),
                S4(A("r4"), A("F_bad"), N(2), A("B")),
                S4(A("r5"), A("F_wide"), N(1), A("A")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("F_ok"),
             S2(S2(A("a1"), A("b1")), S2(A("a2"), A("b2")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("F_bad"), S2(S1(A("a1")), S1(A("a2")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("F_wide"), S1(S2(A("a1"), A("x"))))))),

"THE PARTITION IGNORES WHERE THE UNIQUENESS SITS, and these two cases answer the same table to prove it. Halpin 10.3: a single-role UC makes the fact type functional FROM the player of the role it spans, so it absorbs into THAT player's table. With the UC on role 1, F absorbing into A is right. With it on role 2, F is functional from B and must absorb into B -- and canon answers A either way. FIXED: role 1 answers A and role 2 answers B. The second case pinned the wrong answer deliberately until it did, so the fix arrived as a diff rather than as a claim. Everything needed is already present: the store records the position (the spans cell, 406 rows in cancel-service, <Role_inv_uc, 2>), system:rmap_role2 already computes the role-2 side, and system:rmap_oneone already reads spans. system:rmap_side now consults it: its outer COND -- the branch taken by every plain single-role UC, which always returned the role-1 subject -- asks system:rmap_uc2 first and takes the role-2 table when the uniqueness spans role 2. The 1:1 branch is untouched; Halpin absorbs a 1:1 into its mandatory side and that was always right. Measured cost: NINE apps collapse Fact_Type_has_Role by half -- 708 to 356, 820 to 396, 750 to 378 -- because its uniqueness is on Role and it absorbs into Fact Type anyway, where one column cannot hold many roles per fact type.",
DEF("case:partition-uc-role1",
    S2(A("system:partition"),
       S5(S3(A("CELL"), A("factType"), S1(S2(A("F"), A("{0} has {1}")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("role"),
             S2(S4(A("r1"), A("F"), N(1), A("A")),
                S4(A("r2"), A("F"), N(2), A("B")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("instanceOf"),
             S2(S2(A("A"), A("ObjectType")), S2(A("B"), A("ObjectType")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("constraint"),
             S1(S4(A("F_uc"), A("uniqueness"), A("F"), A("alethic")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("spans"), S1(S2(A("F_uc"), N(1))))))),

DEF("case:partition-uc-role2",
    S2(A("system:partition"),
       S5(S3(A("CELL"), A("factType"), S1(S2(A("F"), A("{0} has {1}")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("role"),
             S2(S4(A("r1"), A("F"), N(1), A("A")),
                S4(A("r2"), A("F"), N(2), A("B")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("instanceOf"),
             S2(S2(A("A"), A("ObjectType")), S2(A("B"), A("ObjectType")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("constraint"),
             S1(S4(A("F_uc"), A("uniqueness"), A("F"), A("alethic")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("spans"), S1(S2(A("F_uc"), N(2))))))),

"AN OBLIGATION WITH AN ANTECEDENT IS A SUBSET, and that is the shape working practices are written in: \"It is obligatory that if some Agent asserts some Claim then that Agent verifies that Claim against some Source.\" cmd:deontic_viols read only deontic_forbidden -- the population of a forbidden fact type IS its violation set -- so every obligation in the corpus reported nothing. The violations of a subset are the ANTECEDENT rows the consequent does not cover, and they must be compared at the ANTECEDENT's width: here the consequent is wider (it names the Source too), and comparing whole rows finds nothing and reports a clean board. F_cons is deliberately ternary against a binary antecedent for exactly that reason. Derived over arest-dev this answers 21 violations across three obligations, eight of them claims this agent asserted from unauthoritative sources during the 2026-08-19..24 run.",
DEF("case:deontic-subset-viols",
    S2(A("cmd:subset_viols"),
       S3(S3(A("CELL"), A("constraint"),
             S1(S5(A("sub_x"), A("subset"), A("F_ant"), A("F_cons"),
                   A("deontic")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("F_ant"),
             S2(S2(A("a"), A("b")), S2(A("c"), A("d")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("F_cons"),
             S1(S3(A("a"), A("b"), A("s1"))))))),

DEF("case:render-json-tree",
    S2(A("render:json"),
       S2(A("menu"), S1(S3(A("button"), A("finish"), A("completed")))))),

DEF("case:render-menu-canonical", S2(A("system:render_html"), S2(A("menu"), S1(S3(A("button"), A("go"), A("done")))))),

DEF("case:and-tt", S2(A("and"), S2(A("T"), A("T")))),
DEF("case:and-tf", S2(A("and"), S2(A("T"), A("F")))),
DEF("case:not-t", S2(A("not"), A("T"))),
DEF("case:not-f", S2(A("not"), A("F"))),
DEF("case:le-num", S2(A("le"), S2(N(1), N(2)))),
DEF("case:le-str", S2(A("le"), S2(A("a"), A("b")))),
DEF("case:length", S2(A("length"), S3(A("a"), A("b"), A("c")))),
DEF("case:length-phi", S2(A("length"), PHI())),
DEF("case:chars", S2(A("chars"), A("abc"))),
DEF("case:charup", S2(A("charup"), A("a"))),
DEF("case:charup-noop", S2(A("charup"), A("A"))),
DEF("case:chardown", S2(A("chardown"), A("Z"))),
DEF("case:charisup", S2(A("charisup"), A("A"))),
DEF("case:charisup-f", S2(A("charisup"), A("a"))),
DEF("case:charislow", S2(A("charislow"), A("a"))),
DEF("case:charisdigit", S2(A("charisdigit"), A("7"))),
DEF("case:charisdigit-f", S2(A("charisdigit"), A("x"))),
DEF("case:ntoa", S2(A("ntoa"), N(42))),
DEF("case:quote-str", S2(A("quote_str"), A("ab"))),
DEF("case:strip-prefix", S2(A("strip_prefix"), S2(A("ab"), A("abc")))),
DEF("case:strip-prefix-miss", S2(A("strip_prefix"), S2(A("zz"), A("abc")))),
DEF("case:strip-prefix-equal", S2(A("strip_prefix"), S2(A("abc"), A("abc")))),
DEF("case:charup-multi", S2(A("charup"), A("zebra"))),
DEF("case:charisup-multi", S2(A("charisup"), A("Zebra"))),
DEF("case:charup-empty", S2(A("charup"), A(""))),
DEF("case:show-int", S2(A("system:show"), N(42))),
DEF("case:show-str", S2(A("system:show"), A("ab"))),
DEF("case:show-numstr", S2(A("system:show"), A("42"))),
DEF("case:show-seq", S2(A("system:show"), S2(A("a"), N(1)))),
DEF("case:show-phi", S2(A("system:show"), PHI())),
DEF("case:show-one", S2(A("system:show"), S1(A("c")))),
DEF("case:show-nested", S2(A("system:show"), S2(S2(A("a"), N(1)), N(2)))),

"THE TRANSACTION, cross-lineage for the first time. create is Def 8 -- resolve, derive to the stratified fixpoint, validate, then commit or roll back -- and cmd:answer is that definition verbatim: COND(null.2, K('committed'), K('refused')) with the new state on commit and the OLD state on refusal, which is D' = D_r[FILE -> P''] if V_A is empty else D. It is exercised by law:apply (the law report calls it command-commit) and by law:exclusion, so it is certified host-to-host. It has NEVER been in the case table, and the case table is the only oracle that spans BOTH lineages -- the law report compares hosts to hosts and the kernel differentials compare engines to engines, so a commit that diverged between the two would have been invisible. The fixture is law:apply's own, not a new one: F1 carries a uniqueness constraint on role 1 and is populated with F1(a,x), so adding F1(a,y) must refuse on Thm 1 and adding F1(b,y) must commit. 1.create takes the verdict off cmd:answer's four-tuple, the same projection law:apply uses.",

DEF("case:create-commits",
    S2(S3(A("COMP"), N(1), A("create")),
       S2(S1(S2(A("F1"), S1(S2(A("b"), A("y"))))),
          S2(S1(S5(A("F1"), S2(A("Thing"), A("Widget")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(), S1(S2(A("a"), A("x"))))), PHI())))),

DEF("case:create-refuses",
    S2(S3(A("COMP"), N(1), A("create")),
       S2(S1(S2(A("F1"), S1(S2(A("a"), A("y"))))),
          S2(S1(S5(A("F1"), S2(A("Thing"), A("Widget")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(), S1(S2(A("a"), A("x"))))), PHI())))),

"THE TRANSLATOR ARBITRATION TABLE, cross-lineage. tr-kinds evaluates the whole 44-row population so the ORDER is pinned per host and not merely its membership -- the order decides which production claims a statement, and class_rule standing before rule_if in the derivation-rule row is the reason a quoted-head classification statement is not read as a rule and made to mint a fact type per value. kinds-of and kinds-of-cardinality select one translator out of it PRESERVING that order rather than sorting, which would silently re-rank the productions. kinds-of-unknown asks for translate_partitions, a name the grammar declares and neither host table ever carried: it answers the EMPTY sequence, the graceful absence a host already spells for a translator it has not registered, and it names the drift this population exists to end.",

DEF("case:tr-kinds", S2(A("system:tr_kinds"), PHI())),

DEF("case:kinds-of", S2(A("system:kinds_of"), S1(A("translate_derivation_rules")))),

DEF("case:kinds-of-cardinality",
    S2(A("system:kinds_of"), S1(A("translate_cardinality_constraints")))),

DEF("case:kinds-of-unknown", S2(A("system:kinds_of"), S1(A("translate_partitions")))),

"THE MODAL OPERATORS, cross-lineage. The table is evaluated whole so the phrases are pinned per host and not merely counted: a host that recognized five of the six, or dropped the trailing space that separates the operator from the statement it opens, would answer a different table here rather than a different compile somewhere later. The LAST ROW carries the empty operator, which is the default: every statement has a modality and an unmarked one is alethic positive, so the table is total and no host needs a fallback of its own.",

DEF("case:modal-ops", S2(A("system:modal_ops"), PHI())),

"THE QUANTIFIER VOCABULARY, cross-lineage. quant-min and quant-full are evaluated whole so the WORDS are pinned per host rather than counted: a host short one word would strip a reading differently and declare a different fact type, which shows up here and not four compile stages later. quant-full is not a literal -- it is quant-min concatenated with the two indefinite articles -- so the case also holds the containment each host had spelled out by hand. qualifiers is the separate list of words that may stand between a quantifier and the type name naming the player.",

DEF("case:quant-min", S2(A("system:quant_min"), PHI())),

DEF("case:quant-full", S2(A("system:quant_full"), PHI())),

DEF("case:qualifiers", S2(A("system:qualifiers"), PHI())),

"THE NOUN-DECIDING VOCABULARIES, cross-lineage. implicit-stop is evaluated whole because its CONTENTS are the meaning: a maximal Title-case run is a noun candidate, so a host short one of these twenty-seven words would mint an entity type called If, and a host carrying one too many would silently drop a real one -- neither shows up as a refusal, only as a different model. noun-quants is the separate list that vouches for a candidate, and it is deliberately NOT quant-min: it carries every and any and drops that, because opening a phrase is a different job from being stripped out of a reading. owned-modes names the two derivation modes whose stored rows the engine may replace, which is what keeps a sweep away from asserted data.",

DEF("case:implicit-stop", S2(A("system:implicit_stop"), PHI())),

DEF("case:noun-quants", S2(A("system:noun_quants"), PHI())),

DEF("case:owned-modes", S2(A("system:owned_modes"), PHI())),

"THE FALLBACK AND OUTPUT VOCABULARIES, cross-lineage. generic-classifications is the pair a specific classification is allowed to beat, so it decides which translator claims a statement; it stood three times, once in python and TWICE in engine/rust as the same const inside two different functions. generated-prefixes names the cell families a recompile replaces whole, which is what keeps a regeneration away from authored cells -- eleven prefixes, pinned here rather than counted, because a host one short would strand a stale family and one too long would delete something nobody generated. sm-phrases is what makes a malformed transition LOUD instead of absent.",

DEF("case:generic-classifications",
    S2(A("system:generic_classifications"), PHI())),

DEF("case:generated-prefixes", S2(A("system:generated_prefixes"), PHI())),

DEF("case:sm-phrases", S2(A("system:sm_phrases"), PHI())),

"THE COOK BOUNDARY, cross-lineage. cooked-kinds is evaluated whole because the MEMBERSHIP is the meaning: a kind in this list reaches its handler already resolved, and a kind missing from it arrives raw and compiles to something else entirely. Twenty-one rows -- engine/rust held twenty and carried the twenty-first, rule_iff, as a disjunct beside the membership test, which is what a list looks like after it has drifted once and been patched rather than corrected. cook-exceptions is the one pair that opts back out: a fact type reading stated deontically takes the deontic transform instead, because the obligation is about the reading and not about the fact it would assert.",

DEF("case:cooked-kinds", S2(A("system:cooked_kinds"), PHI())),

DEF("case:cook-exceptions", S2(A("system:cook_exceptions"), PHI())),

"THE VERB SURFACE, cross-lineage. The three lists are evaluated whole because the CONTENTS are the interface: what the system says it can do. engine/rust advertised eleven session verbs where python has twenty-two and thirteen app verbs where python has sixteen, and used the short lists to decide whether a verb existed at all, so the resident answered unknown op for validate and for every tutor verb. resident-ops is the subset a store answers alone, and it had three different answers in one file -- an array of eight, an error string naming five, and nine arms implemented. Pinned here, one list each, and the hosts agree on them the way they agree on everything else.",

DEF("case:session-verbs", S2(A("system:session_verbs"), PHI())),

DEF("case:app-verbs", S2(A("system:app_verbs"), PHI())),

DEF("case:resident-ops", S2(A("system:resident_ops"), PHI())),

"THE STORE BOUNDARY, cross-lineage. store-verbs is what a STORE alone answers, so it is the line between what can ship to a browser as wasm and what needs a registry behind it -- one dispatch, two bindings, and this names which verbs the two bindings share. read-family is the four whose fast route can be killed so they resolve through the delegate reference instead; it stood twice in engine/rust, in the kill switch and in the path the switch gates. receipt-verbs is which verbs mutate, and so leave a receipt for context to replay -- two, and now sayable rather than only inferable from where a field gets assigned.",

DEF("case:store-verbs", S2(A("system:store_verbs"), PHI())),

DEF("case:read-family", S2(A("system:read_family"), PHI())),

DEF("case:receipt-verbs", S2(A("system:receipt_verbs"), PHI())),

"THE READ SIGNATURES, cross-lineage. read-verbs is which verbs read a store without changing it, and read-args is what each one takes after the app, IN ORDER -- get wants a noun then an id, and the other way round is a different call that still type-checks, so the order is the contract and not a detail of presentation. The two are kept apart on purpose: a verb with no rows in read-args takes no arguments, which is a different answer from a verb nobody has heard of, and cli.py needs to tell those apart to know whether to run it at all. Arity is the count of a verb s rows plus one for the app, so nothing restates it.",

DEF("case:read-verbs", S2(A("system:read_verbs"), PHI())),

DEF("case:read-args", S2(A("system:read_args"), PHI())),

"THE MODAL PREFIX, cross-lineage, and it is a DERIVATION rather than a table. Verbalizing puts back the opening a statement was stripped of, so the prefix for a modality and a sign is the operator that means them -- system:modal_ops read backwards, selecting the LAST row that matches. The last is what makes alethic positive verbalize as nothing rather than as It is necessary that, because modal_ops carries the unmarked default after the marked form. modal-prefix-possible is the row python never had: its dict covered four of the six pairs and raised out of nf for the other two. deontic-ops is which operator name a deontic constraint takes by sign, which both hosts spelled as a conditional next to a second conditional choosing the prefix.",

DEF("case:modal-prefix-plain",
    S2(A("system:modal_prefix"), S2(A("alethic"), A("positive")))),

DEF("case:modal-prefix-deontic",
    S2(A("system:modal_prefix"), S2(A("deontic"), A("negative")))),

DEF("case:modal-prefix-possible",
    S2(A("system:modal_prefix"), S2(A("deontic"), A("possibility")))),

DEF("case:deontic-ops", S2(A("system:deontic_ops"), PHI())),

"THE DERIVATION MODES, cross-lineage. NORMA s four markers, what each MEANS, and whether it materializes -- one fact type where the hosts had three tables and a match between them. Evaluated whole because the ORDER is load-bearing: longest marker first, since a scanner that tries * before ** reads a stored derivation as a computed one and silently stops storing its rows, which is a wrong answer nobody is told about. The third column is the cut python kept in engine.py while the first two lived in compiler.py, and it is a DIFFERENT cut from system:owned_modes: materialize is ** and ++, whose rows get written down, owned is * and **, whose rows the engine may replace.",

DEF("case:derivation-modes", S2(A("system:derivation_modes"), PHI())),

"THE CLAUSE VOCABULARIES, cross-lineage. cmp-ops is which English phrase means which comparison, so a clause saying is at least compiles to ge and not to gt. It stood three times: a python dict, the alternation inside the python regex that RECOGNIZES the clause, and a rust array -- and the two python copies were the dangerous pair, because a phrase present in the regex and missing from the dict raises out of a statement the parser has just accepted. agg-ops is the five an aggregate clause may name, held in an alternation on one side and an array on the other. Both regexes are built from these rows now, so the recognizer and the map cannot disagree about what the language contains.",

DEF("case:cmp-ops", S2(A("system:cmp_ops"), PHI())),

DEF("case:agg-ops", S2(A("system:agg_ops"), PHI())),

"THE CONSTRAINT PREFIXES AND THE BOOTSTRAP KINDS, cross-lineage. cs-prefix is what a set-comparison constraint s minted id opens with, so a reader tells an inclusive-or from a subset from an equality at a glance and the ids sort together -- three rows, held as a python dict beside a rust match, with the empty prefix as the fallthrough on both sides. bootstrap-kinds is the five the SEED compiler dispatches, which is the entire surface of the kernel that has to compile the grammar file before the grammar file can classify anything; it was a python set and nothing else could name it.",

DEF("case:cs-prefix", S2(A("system:cs_prefix"), PHI())),

DEF("case:bootstrap-kinds", S2(A("system:bootstrap_kinds"), PHI())),

"THE CONNECTIVES, cross-lineage, and there are TWO of them. hop-connectives is which words open a step in a role path, so drives some Car that is fast reads as two hops rather than one predicate with an odd name. anaphor-connectives is the pair introducing a back-reference to the subject already named, and it carries THOSE where the hop pair carries WHO -- a site that confuses them classifies an inverse uniqueness constraint as something else, which is why they are two defs and not one list of three words. cardinality-openers sits beside the anaphoric pair in the same statement and the same regex. The lengths a scanner needs come from the words now, not from a hand-counted 6 and 7 that a third connective would strand.",

DEF("case:hop-connectives", S2(A("system:hop_connectives"), PHI())),

DEF("case:anaphor-connectives", S2(A("system:anaphor_connectives"), PHI())),

DEF("case:cardinality-openers", S2(A("system:cardinality_openers"), PHI())),

"THE ATTACHMENT MODES, cross-lineage. Which WAY a constraint kind reaches a cell s validate -- local over the target population alone, foreign over the population with the store so siblings can be fetched, arc for the mandatory family s second named remainder, ab for an equality s two sides, clause for one attachment per clause the target appears in. The BODIES are host closures and stay so; what was held twice is the MEMBERSHIP, as a python dict s keys and as five or-lists of kind names in engine/rust. The last two rows are the deontic pair: python attaches them and engine/rust has no arm, so a deontic check that flags on one host is silent on the other. Naming the modes is what turns that from an anonymous wildcard into something a reader can see.",

DEF("case:attach-modes", S2(A("system:attach_modes"), PHI())),

"THE SET-COMPARISON BUILDERS, cross-lineage. Which canon object an attachment spec names, and so which of the constraints: family actually runs when an exclusion or a scoped subset reaches a cell. Eight rows, held as a python dict of lambdas and eight match arms in engine/rust, each pairing the spec with the builder and then shaping its operand -- the shapes stay host, since they read positions off the A-row. Every row here is constraints: followed by the spec name, and the pairs are written out ANYWAY, because a convention that is only ever computed cannot be checked, cannot take an exception, and cannot be read by anything that does not already know it. The coverage gate checks each name resolves to a def that exists.",

DEF("case:cs-builders", S2(A("system:cs_builders"), PHI())),

"THE PANE MODEL, cross-lineage. panes is iFactr s pane-addressed view stack in ORDINAL order -- a pane later in the list sits above one earlier when both are shown, so the order is layout and not presentation of the list. pane-for is which pane a frame KIND opens in: a list is the master layer, an entity view the detail, an entry form the popover because it is modal, the noun set the tabs. This one closes no gap between the two rust lineages -- it lived in one python file and nowhere else -- but a UI decision expressed as data is the only form a second platform can honor, and every host agreeing on these eight atoms is what makes a target other than python possible at all.",

DEF("case:panes", S2(A("system:panes"), PHI())),

DEF("case:pane-for", S2(A("system:pane_for"), PHI())),

"TWO LOCAL VALIDATORS ON ONE FACT TYPE, which is the shape engine/rust cannot reduce. Every other validate-of case above passes a local list of ONE. The identity app carries a fact type with two -- User_has_Username_uc at span 1 and the inverse Username_inv_uc at span 2 -- and engine/rust s native validate answers 0 parts on it in six seconds with fuel unbounded, where python answers three. This case asks the same question of the hosts: if they agree and answer both violations, canon composes two locals correctly and the fault is in how engine/rust BUILDS the operand; if a host refuses or they disagree, the fault is in the reduction and every host has it. The case is the discriminator, not a guess about which.",

DEF("case:validate-of-two-local", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:validate_of")),
                K(S4(S2(S2(A("CONST"), S1(S1(A("x")))),
                        S2(A("CONST"), S1(S1(A("y"))))),
                     PHI(), PHI(), PHI())))),
          A("id"))),
    S2(S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b"))), PHI()))),
"FOUR CASES PIN derive:cannot_produce, because the case gate passed TWO wrong versions of it: one that silently dropped every aggregate, and one that skipped a rule whose right-hand source had changed. Coverage is structural -- nothing in the table fired a count rule or a join rule with an empty left delta, so nothing complained; law rules-reproduce is what caught the second, on a transitive closure that stopped closing. The guard is a decision, so these test the decision rather than reconstructing a whole firing.",
DEF("case:cannot-produce-empty", S2(A("derive:cannot_produce"),
    S2(S2(A("H"), S3(A("flat"), A("F1"), PHI())),
       S2(PHI(), PHI())))),
DEF("case:cannot-produce-fires", S2(A("derive:cannot_produce"),
    S2(S2(A("H"), S3(A("flat"), A("F1"), PHI())),
       S2(PHI(), S1(S2(A("F1"), S1(S2(A("a"), A("b"))))))))),
"AN EMPTY DELTA ON A COUNT RULE MUST STILL FIRE: COUNT over an empty relation is 0, which is a fact, not the absence of one.",
DEF("case:cannot-produce-count-fires", S2(A("derive:cannot_produce"),
    S2(S2(A("H"), S3(A("count"), A("F1"), PHI())),
       S2(PHI(), PHI())))),
"A JOIN MUST FIRE WHEN EITHER SIDE CHANGED, and this is the case that a whole law turned on. joinon and the join fallback carry BOTH delta directions in one arm -- srcd(left) against srcf(right), then srcf(left) against srcd(right) -- so a test that looks only at the left delta skips a rule whose RIGHT side changed, and a transitive closure stops closing. Here F1 has no delta and F2 does.",
DEF("case:cannot-produce-join-fires", S2(A("derive:cannot_produce"),
    S2(S2(A("H"), S4(A("join"), A("F1"), A("F2"), S1(N(1)))),
       S2(PHI(), S1(S2(A("F2"), S1(S2(A("a"), A("b"))))))))),
"AND IT IS SKIPPABLE ONLY WHEN BOTH SIDES ARE STILL. Neither source has a delta, so neither term of the arm can contribute a row.",
DEF("case:cannot-produce-join-both-still", S2(A("derive:cannot_produce"),
    S2(S2(A("H"), S4(A("join"), A("F1"), A("F2"), S1(N(1)))),
       S2(PHI(), PHI())))),
"TWO CASES PIN derive:any_reads, the test that decides whether a create needs a fixpoint at all. A wrong F here does not fail loudly -- it silently leaves facts underived -- and this session watched the case gate pass two wrong versions of the sibling test, so the decision is pinned directly. The rule set is one join reading F1 and F2.",
DEF("case:any-reads-hit", S2(A("derive:any_reads"),
    S2(S1(A("F2")),
       S1(S2(A("H"), S4(A("join"), A("F1"), A("F2"), S1(N(1)))))))),
"AND A CELL NO RULE READS CANNOT MAKE ONE FIRE, which is the whole saving: the store is already at its fixpoint by law derive-fixpoint, so if no source changed, no rule can produce.",
DEF("case:any-reads-miss", S2(A("derive:any_reads"),
    S2(S1(A("F9")),
       S1(S2(A("H"), S4(A("join"), A("F1"), A("F2"), S1(N(1)))))))),
"mcp:tools IS NOT PINNED BY A CASE, and that is a real gap rather than an oversight. The two cases that stood here pinned a hand-written verb table -- a count and a first row -- and the table is gone: the tool surface is now derived from the store, so pinning it needs a store, and a case operand is a literal. What checks it today is only the server answering 24 tools. Everything this session learned about structural coverage says that is not enough."
,
"THE FORM TABLE CLOSES THE TAG SET, and these pin it. delta_eval used to be a COND chain whose final else was the join, so a recipe form nobody recognized did not refuse -- it QUIETLY BECAME A JOIN. That is how a transitive closure stopped closing this session, caught by law rules-reproduce and by none of the cases. Now the tag resolves through derive:forms or it resolves to nothing.",
DEF("case:form-lookup", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:find_desc"),
       S3(A("CONS"), S2(A("CONST"), A("joinon")), A("derive:forms"))),
    PHI())),
"AND A FORM THAT NAMES NOTHING REFUSES RATHER THAN JOINING. This case is one of the deliberate refusals: it is here to fail loudly if the fallback ever comes back.",
DEF("case:unknown-form-refuses", S2(A("derive:delta_eval"),
    S2(S2(A("bogus"), A("F1")), S2(PHI(), PHI())))),
"A TRIGGER FACT MOVES THE ENTITY, AND ONLY FROM THE RIGHT STATUS. main:st_fired is the step the fold applies once per transition: given a transition and a store it answers the entities the event moved. The second case is the guard -- both sales carry SaleIsSubmitted, but o2 is already placed and a submit leaves draft, so o2 must NOT move. Without it the case would pass on an implementation that moved every entity holding the event fact.",
DEF("case:trigger-advances", S2(A("main:st_fired"), S2(S3(A("draft"), A("Sale is submitted"), A("placed")), S2(S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("SaleIsSubmitted"), S1(S1(A("o1"))))), S1(S2(A("o1"), A("draft"))))))),
DEF("case:trigger-guarded", S2(A("main:st_fired"), S2(S3(A("draft"), A("Sale is submitted"), A("placed")), S2(S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("SaleIsSubmitted"), S2(S1(A("o1")), S1(A("o2"))))), S2(S2(A("o1"), A("draft")), S2(A("o2"), A("placed"))))))),
"COMPILE OUTPUT THAT LANDS WHERE THE TOOL SURFACE READS. The first two pin the compiled shape: a reading names its own fact type and its own players, the capitalized words being the object types. The third is the landing itself -- a two-cell store, one declared fact type, and a reading compiled INTO it, answered through mcp:tools so the assertion is what a caller would actually be offered. Without it the compile could be correct and still write nowhere, which is exactly the state this replaced.",
DEF("case:compile-declares", S2(A("compile:declared_of"), A("Law is scoped to Domain"))),
DEF("case:compile-fts", S2(A("compile:fts_of"), A("Claim is witnessed by Stage"))),
DEF("case:declare-lands", S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("mcp:tools"), A("main:declare")), S2(S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:declared"), S1(S1(S2(A("AHasB"), S2(A("A"), A("B")))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:fts"), S1(S1(S5(A("AHasB"), S2(A("Function"), A("Function")), S1(S2(N(1), N(2))), PHI(), S1(PHI())))))), A("Law holds")))),
"A CREATE WRITES WHERE THE DERIVE PATH READS. main:cf_entry is the swap: the state:fts entry for a fact type keeps its kinds, its uniqueness and its mandatory flag, and takes the new population. The second case is the one that matters -- an entry for a DIFFERENT fact type must come back untouched, since a swap that rewrote every entry would pass the first case and destroy the schema.",
DEF("case:create-swaps-population", S2(A("main:cf_entry"), S2(S5(A("AHasB"), S2(A("Function"), A("Function")), S1(S2(N(1), N(2))), PHI(), S1(S1(S2(A("x"), A("1"))))), S2(A("AHasB"), S2(S2(A("x"), A("1")), S2(A("y"), A("2"))))))),
DEF("case:create-leaves-others", S2(A("main:cf_entry"), S2(S5(A("AHasB"), S2(A("Function"), A("Function")), S1(S2(N(1), N(2))), PHI(), S1(S1(S2(A("x"), A("1"))))), S2(A("CHasD"), S2(S2(A("x"), A("1")), S2(A("y"), A("2"))))))),
"SCHEMA EXTENSION IS ADDRESSED, AND GATED. A POST to DEFS compiles a reading; these two are the gate cor:closure attaches to it. The first store is clean and the step commits. The second holds a population that violates its own uniqueness -- one key, two values -- and the step REFUSES with 409, carrying the violations and no new store, because a refused step makes no D-prime. Without the second the gate would be a branch nothing takes: a fact-type extension adds no rules and starts empty, so neither admission nor validation can fail on the extension ITSELF; what they catch is a population the extended schema would already contradict.",
DEF("case:defs-step-admits", S2(S3(A("COMP"), N(2), A("main:declare_step")), S2(S3(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:declared"), S1(S1(S2(A("AHasB"), S2(A("A"), A("B")))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:rules"), PHI()), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:fts"), S1(S1(S5(A("AHasB"), S2(A("Function"), A("Function")), S1(S2(N(1), N(2))), PHI(), S1(PHI())))))), A("Law holds")))),
DEF("case:defs-step-refuses", S2(S3(A("COMP"), N(2), A("main:declare_step")), S2(S3(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:declared"), S1(S1(S2(A("AHasB"), S2(A("A"), A("B")))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:rules"), PHI()), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:fts"), S1(S1(S5(A("AHasB"), S2(A("Function"), A("Function")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(), S1(S2(S2(A("x"), A("1")), S2(A("x"), A("2"))))))))), A("Law holds")))),
"A CREATE IS VALIDATED, AND THE FLAG IS WHAT REFUSES IT. main:validate_leg answers <population, violations, refuse?> and build_system has always tested that third element -- it was pinned to F only because the slot was PHI and ast:stub_validate answered for it. These two are the flag: the same fact type, one uniqueness constraint on role 1, two rows that respect it and two that do not. Without the second the leg would be a validator that never says no.",
DEF("case:create-validates-clean", S2(S3(A("COMP"), N(3), A("main:validate_leg")), S2(A("AHasB"), S2(S2(S2(A("x"), A("1")), S2(A("y"), A("2"))), S3(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:declared"), S1(S1(S2(A("AHasB"), S2(A("A"), A("B")))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:rules"), PHI()), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:fts"), S1(S1(S5(A("AHasB"), S2(A("Function"), A("Function")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(), S1(PHI())))))))))),
DEF("case:create-refuses-alethic", S2(S3(A("COMP"), N(3), A("main:validate_leg")), S2(A("AHasB"), S2(S2(S2(A("x"), A("1")), S2(A("x"), A("2"))), S3(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:declared"), S1(S1(S2(A("AHasB"), S2(A("A"), A("B")))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:rules"), PHI()), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:fts"), S1(S1(S5(A("AHasB"), S2(A("Function"), A("Function")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(), S1(PHI())))))))))),
"A RETRACTED FACT LEAVES, AND AN ABSENT ONE CHANGES NOTHING. main:retract_rows is the step a DELETE applies: the population minus the named fact, through ui:removefirst. The second case is the guard -- retracting a row that is not there must answer the population unchanged, not empty it and not raise, which is what makes a delete safe to repeat.",
DEF("case:retract-removes", S2(A("main:retract_rows"), S3(S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("AHasB"), S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("x"), S1(A("1"))), S3(A("CELL"), A("y"), S1(A("2")))))))), A("AHasB"), S2(A("x"), A("1"))))),
DEF("case:retract-absent", S2(A("main:retract_rows"), S3(S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("AHasB"), S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("x"), S1(A("1"))), S3(A("CELL"), A("y"), S1(A("2")))))))), A("AHasB"), S2(A("z"), A("9"))))),
"A REPLACED FACT TAKES THE OLD ONE-S PLACE, AND A NEW KEY IS ADDED. main:replace_rows is the step a PUT applies: the population with any row agreeing on the uniqueness key dropped, and the new fact in front. The first case replaces x and leaves y alone -- if the key comparison were wrong it would drop both or neither. The second is the upsert: a key that holds no row adds rather than replacing, which is what makes PUT total.",
DEF("case:replace-swaps", S2(A("main:replace_rows"), S3(S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("AHasB"), S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("x"), S1(A("1"))), S3(A("CELL"), A("y"), S1(A("2"))))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:fts"), S1(S1(S5(A("AHasB"), S2(A("Function"), A("Function")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(), S1(S2(S2(A("x"), A("1")), S2(A("y"), A("2"))))))))), A("AHasB"), S2(A("x"), A("9"))))),
DEF("case:replace-upserts", S2(A("main:replace_rows"), S3(S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("AHasB"), S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("x"), S1(A("1"))), S3(A("CELL"), A("y"), S1(A("2"))))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:fts"), S1(S1(S5(A("AHasB"), S2(A("Function"), A("Function")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(), S1(S2(S2(A("x"), A("1")), S2(A("y"), A("2"))))))))), A("AHasB"), S2(A("z"), A("3"))))),
"AND THE SUPERSESSION LAW DISCRIMINATES. A law that answers T is worth nothing until something makes it answer F, and both arms are cheap enough to look like they could never fire. FILE carries the population, so a small store is three cells. catches-live retires the name a while the same store still defines a cell called a -- that is the finiteness_check mistake in miniature, a sentence about a definition outliving the definition. catches-chain retires a and then retires b, which a supersedes -- two hops, so the memory would answer with a name you have to ask about again. holds is the control: the same shape with nothing wrong answers T, which is what stops the other two from passing for the wrong reason.",
DEF("case:supersession-holds", S2(A("law:supersession"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("FunctionIsSupersededByFunction"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("a"), S1(A("b")))))))))),
DEF("case:supersession-catches-live", S2(A("law:supersession"), S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("FunctionIsSupersededByFunction"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("a"), S1(A("b"))))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("a"), PHI())))),
DEF("case:supersession-catches-chain", S2(A("law:supersession"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("FunctionIsSupersededByFunction"), S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("a"), S1(A("b"))), S3(A("CELL"), A("b"), S1(A("c")))))))))),
"AND THE THIRD OUTCOME IS REACHABLE. AREST.tex:189 -- an alethic c rejects the commit, a deontic c warns and commits -- and http:status has declared committed_with_violations -> 200 since before anything could produce it. The five below pin every arm: no violations commits; a lone deontic violation commits WITH them; an alethic one refuses, whether it is a uniqueness or a mandatory; a deontic beside an alethic still refuses, because the gate is a function of the ALETHIC half and one is enough; and a family canon does not know refuses rather than warns, which is the arm worth stating twice -- main:alethic_of filters the deontic ones OUT instead of filtering the alethic ones IN, so an unrecognised modality is treated as hard. Getting that backwards would let an unknown constraint downgrade itself to a warning by being unknown.",
DEF("case:outcome-committed", S2(A("main:create_outcome"), S1(S2(PHI(), PHI())))),
DEF("case:outcome-deontic-commits", S2(A("main:create_outcome"), S1(S2(PHI(), S1(S3(A("AHasB"), A("deontic"), S1(A("x")))))))),
DEF("case:outcome-uniqueness-refuses", S2(A("main:create_outcome"), S1(S2(PHI(), S1(S3(A("AHasB"), A("uniqueness"), S1(A("x")))))))),
DEF("case:outcome-mandatory-refuses", S2(A("main:create_outcome"), S1(S2(PHI(), S1(S3(A("AHasB"), A("mandatory"), S1(A("x")))))))),
DEF("case:outcome-mixed-refuses", S2(A("main:create_outcome"), S1(S2(PHI(), S2(S3(A("AHasB"), A("deontic"), S1(A("x"))), S3(A("AHasB"), A("uniqueness"), S1(A("y")))))))),
DEF("case:outcome-unknown-family-refuses", S2(A("main:create_outcome"), S1(S2(PHI(), S1(S3(A("AHasB"), A("nosuchfamily"), S1(A("x")))))))),
"CONSTRAINT ROLE FILLING, ON SAMUEL-S OWN CASE. A message arriving in a phone system is put on a customer-s history and associated with an agent by the numbers it carries, and agents are NOT identified by phone -- they have one, and sometimes share it. The population below is <agent, phone> with bob and cal sharing 555-0200, which is the whole point: 555-0100 resolves because one agent holds it, 555-0200 does NOT because two do, and an absent number resolves to nothing. AMBIGUITY IS A NON-MATCH RATHER THAN AN ERROR -- the message goes unattributed, because picking one of two customers silently corrupts a history and nothing downstream could detect it. The last case is the one that would have been wrong without dedup: one agent reached by two rows is one candidate, not two, and must still resolve.",
DEF("case:match-unique", S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("match:resolve"), A("match:candidates")), S4(S4(S2(A("ann"), A("555-0100")), S2(A("bob"), A("555-0200")), S2(A("cal"), A("555-0200")), S2(A("dee"), A("555-0300"))), N(2), A("555-0100"), N(1)))),
DEF("case:match-shared-phone-refuses", S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("match:resolve"), A("match:candidates")), S4(S4(S2(A("ann"), A("555-0100")), S2(A("bob"), A("555-0200")), S2(A("cal"), A("555-0200")), S2(A("dee"), A("555-0300"))), N(2), A("555-0200"), N(1)))),
DEF("case:match-absent-number", S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("match:resolve"), A("match:candidates")), S4(S4(S2(A("ann"), A("555-0100")), S2(A("bob"), A("555-0200")), S2(A("cal"), A("555-0200")), S2(A("dee"), A("555-0300"))), N(2), A("555-0999"), N(1)))),
DEF("case:match-dedups-one-agent", S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("match:resolve"), A("match:candidates")), S4(S2(S2(A("ann"), A("555-0100")), S2(A("ann"), A("555-0100"))), N(2), A("555-0100"), N(1)))),
"SUBSET PROPOSES, EQUALITY DISPOSES, END TO END. The four above stop at the propose step; these run the whole chain -- match:candidates, then match:prune, then match:resolve -- on the same shared-phone population. 555-0200 is held by both bob and cal, so the subset arm alone answers nothing. Pruning against the second list is the equality arm: not-in-B entails not-in-A, so cal survives and bob is struck, and the match that could not resolve now does. That is why both arms were needed rather than the ponens half alone -- elimination is the operation that turns two candidates into one. The other two are the guards: a prune that removes EVERYTHING answers nothing rather than falling back to the unpruned candidates, and a prune that leaves an already-unique candidate alone must not disturb it.",
DEF("case:match-prune-resolves-ambiguity", S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("match:resolve"), S3(A("COMP"), A("match:prune"), S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("match:candidates"), S5(A("CONS"), N(1), N(2), N(3), N(4))), N(5)))), S5(S4(S2(A("ann"), A("555-0100")), S2(A("bob"), A("555-0200")), S2(A("cal"), A("555-0200")), S2(A("dee"), A("555-0300"))), N(2), A("555-0200"), N(1), S2(A("cal"), A("dee"))))),
DEF("case:match-prune-can-empty", S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("match:resolve"), S3(A("COMP"), A("match:prune"), S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("match:candidates"), S5(A("CONS"), N(1), N(2), N(3), N(4))), N(5)))), S5(S4(S2(A("ann"), A("555-0100")), S2(A("bob"), A("555-0200")), S2(A("cal"), A("555-0200")), S2(A("dee"), A("555-0300"))), N(2), A("555-0100"), N(1), S2(A("cal"), A("dee"))))),
DEF("case:match-prune-keeps-singleton", S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("match:resolve"), S3(A("COMP"), A("match:prune"), S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("match:candidates"), S5(A("CONS"), N(1), N(2), N(3), N(4))), N(5)))), S5(S4(S2(A("ann"), A("555-0100")), S2(A("bob"), A("555-0200")), S2(A("cal"), A("555-0200")), S2(A("dee"), A("555-0300"))), N(2), A("555-0300"), N(1), S2(A("cal"), A("dee"))))),
"AND THE PATH IS SEARCHED, SO NOTHING HANDS IT POSITIONS. The seven cases above supply keyPos and valPos themselves; match:fill is given only a store, the TYPE it wants, and a value it holds -- which is all a message arriving in a phone system actually has. The search reads state:declared for every fact type carrying a role played by Agent, tries every other position for the number, and proposes whatever sits at the Agent position. 555-0100 resolves, 555-0200 does not because bob and cal share it, and a number nobody holds proposes nothing. The store here is two cells because that is all the search needs: what the fact type declares, and its population.",
DEF("case:match-fill-searches-the-path", S2(A("match:fill"), S3(S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:declared"), S1(S1(S2(A("AgentHasPhone"), S2(A("Agent"), A("Phone")))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("AgentHasPhone"), S4(S2(A("ann"), A("555-0100")), S2(A("bob"), A("555-0200")), S2(A("cal"), A("555-0200")), S2(A("dee"), A("555-0300"))))))), A("Agent"), A("555-0100")))),
DEF("case:match-fill-shared-phone-refuses", S2(A("match:fill"), S3(S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:declared"), S1(S1(S2(A("AgentHasPhone"), S2(A("Agent"), A("Phone")))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("AgentHasPhone"), S4(S2(A("ann"), A("555-0100")), S2(A("bob"), A("555-0200")), S2(A("cal"), A("555-0200")), S2(A("dee"), A("555-0300"))))))), A("Agent"), A("555-0200")))),
DEF("case:match-fill-unknown-number", S2(A("match:fill"), S3(S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:declared"), S1(S1(S2(A("AgentHasPhone"), S2(A("Agent"), A("Phone")))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("AgentHasPhone"), S4(S2(A("ann"), A("555-0100")), S2(A("bob"), A("555-0200")), S2(A("cal"), A("555-0200")), S2(A("dee"), A("555-0300"))))))), A("Agent"), A("555-0999")))),
"AND THE MANDATORY ARM ANSWERS OVER A REAL STORE. Agent has two instances and AgentHasPhone is mandatory on role 1, so an agent with no phone row is a violation and one with a phone is not. The third case is the guard that matters most: with the mandatory slot EMPTY the same store reports nothing, which is what stops this arm from flagging every fact type it walks. The required side is the object type population and the present side is column 1 of the fact type, read through system:pop_rows -- NOT the descriptor-s fifth slot, which would report a violation for all 516 reflected roles, all 268 readings and every derived head, because those live in cells that ast:FetchPop consults before FILE.",
DEF("case:validate-mandatory-fires", S2(A("cmd:mand_viols"), S4(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:fts"), S1(S1(S5(A("AgentHasPhone"), S2(A("Agent"), A("Phone")), S1(S1(N(1))), S1(N(1)), S1(PHI()))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:declared"), S1(S1(S2(A("AgentHasPhone"), S2(A("Agent"), A("Phone")))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:otpops"), S1(S1(S2(A("Agent"), S2(A("ann"), A("bob")))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("AgentHasPhone"), S1(S2(A("ann"), A("555-0100"))))))))),
DEF("case:validate-mandatory-satisfied", S2(A("cmd:mand_viols"), S4(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:fts"), S1(S1(S5(A("AgentHasPhone"), S2(A("Agent"), A("Phone")), S1(S1(N(1))), S1(N(1)), S1(PHI()))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:declared"), S1(S1(S2(A("AgentHasPhone"), S2(A("Agent"), A("Phone")))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:otpops"), S1(S1(S2(A("Agent"), S2(A("ann"), A("bob")))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("AgentHasPhone"), S2(S2(A("ann"), A("555-0100")), S2(A("bob"), A("555-0200"))))))))),
DEF("case:validate-no-mandatory-declared", S2(A("cmd:mand_viols"), S4(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:fts"), S1(S1(S5(A("AgentHasPhone"), S2(A("Agent"), A("Phone")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(), S1(PHI()))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:declared"), S1(S1(S2(A("AgentHasPhone"), S2(A("Agent"), A("Phone")))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:otpops"), S1(S1(S2(A("Agent"), S2(A("ann"), A("bob")))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("AgentHasPhone"), S1(S2(A("ann"), A("555-0100"))))))))),
"AND THE COMMIT ITSELF, WHICH IS THE ONLY PART WORTH TRUSTING. AREST.tex:189 -- an alethic c rejects the commit, a deontic c warns and commits -- and Thm 1 is written on it: D-prime carries P-double-prime iff V has no ALETHIC violation. Both stores below hold ONE violation over the same data; they differ only in its modality, and the write lands in one and not the other. The row count after the create is the assertion, not the status: main:create_outcome answered committed_with_violations and http:status_of answered 200 while main:validate_leg was still refusing on ANY violation, so the API reported success and the store silently did not change. Every check downstream passed. Only counting the rows afterwards found it.",
DEF("case:deontic-violation-commits-the-write", S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("length"), S3(A("COMP"), A("system:pop_rows"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("AgentHasPhone")), A("main:create_closed")))), S3(S5(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:fts"), S1(S1(S5(A("AgentHasPhone"), S2(A("Agent"), A("Phone")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(), S1(PHI()))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:declared"), S1(S1(S2(A("AgentHasPhone"), S2(A("Agent"), A("Phone")))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:otpops"), S1(S1(S2(A("Agent"), S2(A("ann"), A("bob")))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:deontics"), S1(S1(S3(A("DEO:m:AgentHasPhone#1"), A("mandatory"), S1(S2(A("AgentHasPhone"), N(1))))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("AgentHasPhone"), S1(S2(A("ann"), A("555-0100"))))))), A("AgentHasPhone"), S2(A("cal"), A("555-9999"))))),
DEF("case:alethic-violation-refuses-the-write", S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("length"), S3(A("COMP"), A("system:pop_rows"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("AgentHasPhone")), A("main:create_closed")))), S3(S5(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:fts"), S1(S1(S5(A("AgentHasPhone"), S2(A("Agent"), A("Phone")), S1(S1(N(1))), S1(N(1)), S1(PHI()))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:declared"), S1(S1(S2(A("AgentHasPhone"), S2(A("Agent"), A("Phone")))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:otpops"), S1(S1(S2(A("Agent"), S2(A("ann"), A("bob")))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("state:deontics"), S1(PHI())), S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("AgentHasPhone"), S1(S2(A("ann"), A("555-0100"))))))), A("AgentHasPhone"), S2(A("cal"), A("555-9999"))))),
"A PROHIBITED POPULATION IS CHECKED, AND EVERY ROW OF IT IS THE VERDICT (2026-09-09). Three shapes the oracle emits: the fact type itself (every message that uses a dash), a literal restricting the last role (only the separate greeting), and a unary qualifying the last player (only the internal API). An empty population is quiet.",
DEF("case:prohibited-rows-are-violations", S2(A("cmd:deo_viols"), S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:deontics"), S1(S1(S3(A("DEO:p:MessageUsesDash#1"), A("prohibited"), S2(A("MessageUsesDash"), A("MessageUsesDash")))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("MessageUsesDash"), S2(S1(A("m1")), S1(A("m2"))))))))),
DEF("case:prohibited-empty-population-is-quiet", S2(A("cmd:deo_viols"), S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:deontics"), S1(S1(S3(A("DEO:p:MessageUsesDash#1"), A("prohibited"), S2(A("MessageUsesDash"), A("MessageUsesDash")))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("MessageUsesDash"), PHI())))))),
DEF("case:prohibited-literal-restricts-the-role", S2(A("cmd:deo_viols"), S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:deontics"), S1(S1(S3(A("DEO:p:MessageHasOpeningStyle#1"), A("prohibited"), S2(A("MessageHasOpeningStyle"), S4(A("sel"), A("MessageHasOpeningStyle"), N(2), A("separate-greeting"))))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("MessageHasOpeningStyle"), S2(S2(A("m1"), A("separate-greeting")), S2(A("m2"), A("inline"))))))))),
DEF("case:prohibited-qualified-by-a-unary", S2(A("cmd:deo_viols"), S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("state:deontics"), S1(S1(S3(A("DEO:p:MessageRecommendsAPI#1"), A("prohibited"), S2(A("MessageRecommendsAPI"), S5(A("joinon"), A("MessageRecommendsAPI"), A("APIIsInternal"), S1(S2(N(2), N(1))), S2(N(1), N(2)))))))), S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("MessageRecommendsAPI"), S2(S2(A("m1"), A("/ymm/")), S2(A("m2"), A("/vin/")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("APIIsInternal"), S1(S1(A("/ymm/"))))))))),
"THE ENTITY'S MACHINE IS THE MACHINE OF A TYPE IT IS AN INSTANCE OF (2026-09-09). Addressed as a Thing, t1 is a Ticket and Ticket has a machine, so its screen's machine is Ticket's; an id no machine's type holds falls back to the addressed type.",
DEF("case:entity-machine-by-instance-type", S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("ui:machine_type"), S4(A("CONS"), A("fixture:machines"), K(A("Thing")), K(A("t1")))), PHI())),
